    <node id="ALONZO PHELPS">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Alonzo Phelps was a condemned man sentenced to death for murder, who escaped from jail and was subsequently shot and killed by an officer. Despite his criminal record, he is consistently described as a very bad but brave man who openly defied the city of Vicksburg, the law, and the State of Mississippi. His confession, which is read in the text by the character Don Carlo, reveals that Phelps buried the man he murdered and hid a bundle of papers taken from the victim in Europe. These hidden papers later proved crucial in allowing John A. Lasco to claim his family estate. Phelps's confession pamphlet serves as the primary source of these details, painting a portrait of a defiant outlaw whose actions, though violent, had lasting consequences for another man's inheritance.
Relationships:
  - Source: JOHN A. LASCO
  - Target: ALONZO PHELPS
  - Description: Alonzo Phelps's confession led to the discovery of papers that gave John A. Lasco his family estate.</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">2</data>
