**ENTITY: WOLFRED NELSON**
**Description:** Wolfred Nelson was a prominent Canadian rebel leader and physician who played a central role in the Lower Canada Rebellion of 1837–1838. He was the brother of fellow rebel leader Robert Nelson, and the two are often referenced together in historical accounts of the period. Wolfred was an English-speaking member of the Legislative Assembly who acted alongside Louis-Joseph Papineau in the House, and he became one of Papineau's chief lieutenants in the Patriote movement.

Nelson was deeply involved in the organizational and military aspects of the rebellion. He was a key organizer of the St. Ours meeting, where he urged patriots at a public gathering to be ready to arm themselves. As a leader of the Patriotes, he commanded the rebel forces at the Battle of St. Denis, where he achieved a significant victory over British troops. This success inspired rebels in St. Eustache and elsewhere, though it also marked a turning point in the conflict. At St. Denis, Nelson ordered the detention of Lieutenant Weir, a British officer, and later denied having advised Papineau to flee the area, indicating a divergence in their strategies.

Despite his early military success, Nelson was eventually captured by loyalist militia near the frontier and imprisoned in Montreal. He was held as a political prisoner, one of the most important rebel leaders taken during the uprising. Later, as part of Lord Durham's amnesty, Nelson was transported to Bermuda, where he was exiled along with other captured rebels.

Descriptions of Nelson also note that he was, at times, a stranger to the men under his command, suggesting that his leadership was not always based on close personal ties with his troops. He is also mentioned in illustrations of the rebellion, confirming his visual presence in the historical record as a participant and leader.

In summary, Wolfred Nelson was a physician, politician, and military commander who rose to prominence as a leader of the Patriote rebellion. He was a brother of Robert Nelson, a close associate of Papineau, and a decisive figure at St. Denis. His capture, imprisonment, and subsequent exile to Bermuda highlight his central role in the failed uprising and his lasting significance in Canadian revolutionary history.
**Relationships:** (No explicit relationships listed in the data tables for this entity)

