**ENTITY: PROCLAMATION**
**Description:** Robert Nelson issued a proclamation that served multiple, interconnected purposes, primarily centered on declaring the independence of Canada and establishing his own political authority. The document, signed by Nelson, formally declared the independence of Canada, specifically Lower Canada, and was the act by which he was proclaimed President of the Republic of Lower Canada. In addition to this declaration of sovereignty, the proclamation was also issued by Durham—though this appears to be a separate or additional context—to explain his resignation and appeal to Canadian public opinion. Resolving the apparent contradiction, the proclamation is best understood as a dual-purpose document: on one hand, it was Nelson's formal declaration of independence and his assumption of the presidency of the Republic of Lower Canada; on the other, it was used by Durham as a public statement to justify his resignation and to directly engage with the Canadian populace. Thus, the proclamation functioned both as a revolutionary act of nation-building and as a political communication tool, reflecting the turbulent political climate of the period.
**Relationships:** (None listed)

---

