    <node id="NINETY-TWO RESOLUTIONS">
      <data key="d3">7</data>
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">The Ninety-Two Resolutions were a formal list of grievances and political demands adopted by the Patriote party in Lower Canada in 1834. Drafted primarily by Augustin Morin, the resolutions were presented to the Imperial parliament as a comprehensive indictment of British colonial governance. They articulated the political platform of the extreme wing of the Patriote movement, calling for significant political reforms, including greater local autonomy, responsible government, and an end to the oligarchic control of the British-appointed councils. The document also reflected the growing racial and ethnic tensions between the French-Canadian majority and the British minority, framing the conflict as a fundamental struggle for political and cultural survival. The adoption of the resolutions marked a critical escalation in the political crisis, leading to the withdrawal of support from P. D. Debartzch, a former ally of Louis-Joseph Papineau, and ultimately contributing to the Rebellions of 1837–38. The Ninety-Two Resolutions also serve as a chapter title in historical accounts, symbolizing the formal articulation of Lower Canada's grievances and the breakdown of constitutional relations with Britain.
Relationships: None listed</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
