    <node id="ETIENNE P. TACHE">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Étienne P. Taché was a prominent Canadian political figure whose career evolved from local activism to national leadership. Initially known as a local agitator in Montmagny, Quebec, he later became a close colleague of Sir John A. Macdonald and served as prime minister of Canada. Taché was also a faithful follower of Louis-Joseph Papineau, though his support for Papineau occurred outside the formal legislative assembly, indicating his influence and engagement in political circles beyond elected office. His trajectory reflects a transition from grassroots political agitation to high-level governance, ultimately aligning with Macdonald, one of Canada’s founding fathers. Taché’s dual associations—with Papineau’s reformist ideals and Macdonald’s Conservative vision—highlight his adaptability and significance in mid-19th-century Canadian politics. He is remembered as a key figure who bridged local activism and national statesmanship.
Relationships: (none listed in the data)

---</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
