    <node id="EDMUND BAILEY O'CALLAGHAN">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Edmund Bailey O'Callaghan was a prominent figure in the political unrest of Lower Canada during the 1830s. He served as a lieutenant to the influential reform leader Louis-Joseph Papineau, acting as a key ally and supporter in the movement for political change. As an editor of the Montreal Vindicator, a newspaper that championed reformist and nationalist causes, O'Callaghan used his platform to advocate for the rights of the French-Canadian majority and to criticize the colonial government. His close association with Papineau and his active role in the reform movement made him a target for authorities, who issued a warrant for his arrest. To evade capture, O'Callaghan fled and took refuge in the Richelieu district, a region known for its strong support of the Patriote cause. There, he continued his involvement with the movement, aligning himself with Papineau's followers in the countryside. O'Callaghan's career thus combined journalism and political activism, and his actions placed him firmly on the side of the rebels during a period of escalating tension that would eventually lead to the Lower Canada Rebellion of 1837–1838. His legacy is tied to his unwavering support for Papineau and his commitment to the reformist agenda, even at the cost of his own safety.
**Relationships:** (No explicit relationships listed in the provided data for this entity record)</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
