    <node id="FINANCIAL DIFFICULTIES HAD ARISEN BETWEEN THE TWO PROVINCES, AND ADVANTAGE WAS TAKEN OF THIS TO INTRODUCE A UNION BILL INTO THE HOUSE OF COMMONS AT WESTMINSTER, COUCHED IN TERMS VERY UNFAVOURABLE TO THE FRENCH CANADIANS. THE REAL OBJECT OF THE BILL WAS THE EXTINCTION OF THE LOWER-CANADIAN ASSEMBLY AND THE SUBORDINATION OF THE FRENCH TO THE ENGLISH ELEMENT IN THE COLONY [DATA: SOURCES (411); ENTITIES (8953); RELATIONSHIPS (13215)].">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4">
Papineau and John Neilson, the Scottish editor of the Quebec *Gazette*, were appointed as agents to go to London to oppose the bill. They made a very favourable impression and enlisted the leaders of the Whig party on their side [Data: Sources (411); Entities (8953); Relationships (13216, 13217, 13218)].
</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THE PARTY CHAMPIONED FRENCH-CANADIAN RIGHTS AND SOUGHT GREATER DEMOCRATIC CONTROL OVER THE COLONY'S GOVERNANCE. ITS CORE DEMANDS INCLUDED AN ELECTIVE LEGISLATIVE COUNCIL, OPPOSITION TO THE BRITISH-APPOINTED EXECUTIVE AND LEGISLATIVE COUNCILS, AND RESISTANCE TO THE POLICIES OF THE BRITISH GOVERNOR [DATA: ENTITIES (8938); RELATIONSHIPS (13189, 13185)].">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4">
Over time, the Patriotes escalated their goals from democratic reform to outright emancipation from British rule, with younger members issuing manifestos calling for independence and the establishment of a republican government. This radicalization ultimately associated the party with rebellion, culminating in the armed uprisings of 1837–1838 [Data: Entities (8938)].

Papineau was present at the Battle of St. Denis but left early for St. Hyacinthe, and after the defeat at St. Charles, he fled to the Vermont border and eventually to the United States. He had prior military experience as a militia major, which informed his role in the conflict [Data: Entities (8953)].
</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HE PROVED TO BE COMPLETELY OUT OF HARMONY WITH THE NEW ORDER OF THINGS UNDER RESPONSIBLE GOVERNMENT. EVEN WITH HIS OLD LIEUTENANT LAFONTAINE, WHO HAD MADE POSSIBLE HIS RETURN TO CANADA, HE HAD AN OPEN BREACH [DATA: SOURCES (435); ENTITIES (9430)].">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4">
His real importance after his return to Canada lay not in the parliamentary sphere, but in the encouragement he gave to those radical and anti-clerical ideas that found expression in the foundation of the *Institut Canadien* and the formation of the *Parti Rouge*, which was in many respects the continuation of the *Patriote* party of 1837 [Data: Sources (435)].
</data>
    </node>
