    <node id="HOUSE OF ASSEMBLY">
      <data key="d3">9</data>
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">The House of Assembly was the elected lower chamber of the legislature in Lower Canada, serving as the primary political arena for the French-Canadian majority. As a distinct legislative body, it was responsible for debating and passing laws, but its most defining feature was its persistent struggle to gain greater control over colonial finances and government operations. The Assembly frequently clashed with the British-appointed governor and the upper legislative council, as its French-Canadian members—most notably Louis-Joseph Papineau and Jean-Antoine Panet—used their elected positions to challenge executive authority and advocate for responsible government. Through these conflicts, the House of Assembly became the central institution through which French-Canadian political aspirations were expressed, ultimately laying the groundwork for broader constitutional and social tensions in the colony.
Relationships: None listed</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
