    <node id="CHATEAUGUAY">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Châteauguay, a location in Lower Canada, played a significant role during the period of political unrest and military conflict in the early 19th century. It was a gathering point where large bodies of disaffected habitants—French-Canadian farmers and settlers—assembled during the rising, reflecting widespread discontent with colonial governance and economic hardships. This unrest was part of the broader Rebellions of 1837–1838 in Lower Canada, where local populations sought greater political representation and reform.

Crucially, Châteauguay is also historically noted as the site of a battle won by French-Canadian soldiers. This refers to the Battle of the Châteauguay, fought on October 26, 1813, during the War of 1812. In that engagement, a combined force of French-Canadian militia and Mohawk warriors, led by Lieutenant-Colonel Charles-Michel de Salaberry, successfully repelled a much larger American invasion force attempting to advance toward Montreal. The victory was a decisive defensive triumph, preventing the American army from capturing the city and securing the region for British North America.

Thus, Châteauguay holds dual historical significance: as a focal point for civilian unrest and as a battlefield where French-Canadian soldiers demonstrated military prowess and loyalty to the Crown. The location embodies both the internal tensions of colonial society and the external defense of the territory. While the gathering of disaffected habitants occurred during the later rebellion period, the battle took place earlier, yet both events underscore the complex role of French-Canadians in shaping the region's history—sometimes in opposition to authority, and at other times in defense of it. Together, these descriptions highlight Châteauguay as a place of both protest and patriotism, where local populations actively influenced the course of Lower Canada's development.
**Relationships:** (No explicit relationships listed in the data tables for this entity beyond its description.)

---</data>
