    <node id="POLITICALLY, LOWER CANADA WAS CHARACTERIZED BY INTENSE STRUGGLES BETWEEN THE ELECTED LEGISLATIVE ASSEMBLY, WHICH WAS LARGELY FRENCH-CANADIAN, AND THE APPOINTED BRITISH GOVERNOR AND LEGISLATIVE COUNCILS. THE CENTRAL DISPUTES INVOLVED SUPPLY VOTES, THE POWER OF THE PURSE, AND THE DEMAND FOR RESPONSIBLE GOVERNMENT, WITH THE ASSEMBLY SEEKING CONTROL OVER THE EXECUTIVE. THESE TENSIONS WERE EXACERBATED BY THE PRESENTATION OF THE NINETY-TWO RESOLUTIONS, A LIST OF GRIEVANCES, AND THE SUPPRESSION OF THE FRENCH-LANGUAGE NEWSPAPER LE CANADIEN. KEY FIGURES INCLUDED LOUIS-JOSEPH PAPINEAU, WHO WAS ACTIVE IN THE POLITICAL AGITATION, AND GOVERNOR GOSFORD, WHO SERVED DURING THE LEGISLATIVE SESSIONS OF 1835 AND 1836. THE POLITICAL CRISIS ESCALATED INTO OPEN REBELLION, LEADING TO MILITARY DRILLS AND ESCALATING TENSIONS. IN RESPONSE, THE BRITISH GOVERNMENT SUSPENDED THE COLONY'S CONSTITUTION AND SENT LORD DURHAM AS GOVERNOR TO INVESTIGATE AND RESTORE ORDER.">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
