ENTITY: UPPER CANADA
Description: Upper Canada was a British colony in North America, created in 1791 as one of two provinces—the other being Lower Canada—and was predominantly English-speaking. It was a region with its own elected Assembly, though its political life was heavily influenced by the British Crown. Sir Francis Bond Head served as lieutenant-governor, and his instructions to the legislature caused significant political fallout, straining relations between the colonial government and local representatives.

The colony was a central site of the 1837 Rebellions, which were part of a broader uprising that also affected Lower Canada. In Upper Canada, William Lyon Mackenzie led a rebellion, while Thomas Storrow Brown sought to initiate further unrest. The rebellion had a counterpart in Lower Canada and significantly influenced public opinion in Britain regarding colonial governance. The uprising led to severe consequences, including the hanging of rebels Samuel Lount and Peter Matthews.

Upper Canada was also the subject of Lord Durham's Report, which proposed a legislative union with Lower Canada to address ongoing political and ethnic tensions. This recommendation ultimately shaped the future of British North America. The colony's history is marked by its English-speaking majority, its struggles for responsible government, and its role in the larger movement toward Canadian confederation.
Relationships:
