ENTITY: MONTREAL
Description: Montreal, a city in Lower Canada (now Quebec, Canada), was a central hub of political and social unrest during the 1830s. It was the site of the initial fracas between the Doric Club and the Fils de la Liberte, which escalated into rioting on November 6. The Fils de la Liberte association was formed there in autumn 1837, and it was from Montreal that Louis-Joseph Papineau was advised to leave after delivering an address. The city also saw a by-election in 1832 that culminated in a riot involving troops and French Canadians.

Montreal was a stronghold of British loyalist activity, where constitutional associations were formed, a volunteer rifle corps was organized to protect British interests, and loyalist meetings were held. It was also the seat of Mgr Lartigue's bishopric. Loyalists fled to Montreal to report rebel activities, and it served as the base for Sir John Colborne. The city housed political prisoners in its prisons and was the location of the state trials in 1838-39.

Several key figures were associated with Montreal. Dr. Wolfred Nelson was imprisoned there after his capture, and later returned to practice his profession in the city. Thomas Storrow Brown, an iron-merchant in Montreal, also returned to business there after being injured during the rioting. Papineau delivered an address in the city before being advised to leave.

Montreal was a focal point for rebel planning, as insurgents planned to advance on and capture the city as part of their invasion strategy, though it was never taken. The unrest also led to looting, as described in a letter from an inhabitant of St Benoit to a friend. Throughout the period of unrest, Montreal experienced repeated rioting and served as a center for both loyalist organization and rebel agitation, making it a critical location in the Lower Canada Rebellions.
