    <node id="HE WAS PRESENT AT THE BATTLE OF ST. DENIS BUT LEFT EARLY FOR ST. HYACINTHE, AND AFTER THE DEFEAT AT ST. CHARLES, HE FLED TO THE VERMONT BORDER AND EVENTUALLY TO THE UNITED STATES [DATA: ENTITIES (8953); RELATIONSHIPS (14633, 14635, 14636)]. HE HAD PRIOR MILITARY EXPERIENCE AS A MILITIA MAJOR, WHICH INFORMED HIS ROLE IN THE CONFLICT [DATA: ENTITIES (8953)].">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4">
Papineau also had significant interactions with other figures in the movement. He gave credentials to Amury Girod to oversee the cause in the north, and Charles Ovide Perrault was his closest follower and confidant [Data: Entities (8953); Relationships (14478)]. He initially had dealings with Nelson and Cote but later broke off negotiations, possibly after discovering President Van Buren's neutrality [Data: Entities (8953)]. He also broke with Étienne Parent, a fellow political thinker, over differences in approach [Data: Entities (9005, 8953)].
</data>
    </node>
