    <node id="ST OURS">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">ST OURS was a significant site of Patriote activity during the Lower Canada Rebellion of 1837. Located on the Richelieu River, it served as a major gathering point for the movement. On May 7, 1837, the Patriotes held a large open-air meeting there, which was a key demonstration of their political mobilization. The village was also the location where a pole was erected in favor of a dismissed captain, bearing the inscription "Elu par le peuple" (Elected by the people), symbolizing popular support and defiance against colonial authorities. During the rising, St Ours became a rallying point where large bodies of disaffected habitants gathered, indicating widespread local support for the rebellion. The village was held by the Patriotes, and British forces under Colonel Gore avoided it by making a detour, underscoring its strategic importance and the threat it posed to the colonial military. Additionally, after the Battle of St Denis, British troops retreated to St Ours, making it a temporary refuge and a focal point in the military movements of the conflict. Overall, St Ours was a central hub for Patriote organization, popular protest, and military activity during the rebellion.
**Relationships:** (No explicit relationships listed in the data tables for this entity beyond its description.)

---</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
