    <node id="REBEL FORCES UNDER THE COMMAND OF DR. CYRILLE CÔTÉ ADVANCED INTO THE AREA AND INITIALLY DROVE BACK A PICKET OF LOYALIST VOLUNTEERS. HOWEVER, THE LOYALIST MILITIA WAS QUICKLY REINFORCED, AND THE SUBSEQUENT CONFRONTATION PROVED DISASTROUS FOR THE REBELS. THE REINFORCED LOYALIST FORCES MOUNTED A STRONG OPPOSITION, ULTIMATELY DEFEATING CÔTÉ'S REBEL CONTINGENT. THIS DEFEAT EFFECTIVELY CRUSHED THE REBEL UPRISING IN THAT REGION AND CONTRIBUTED TO THE OVERALL FAILURE OF THE SECOND REBELLION [DATA: ENTITIES (9254)].">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4" />
    </node>
    <node id="THIS MARKED A SIGNIFICANT SHIFT IN BRITISH COLONIAL GOVERNANCE [DATA: REPORTS (3); ENTITIES (8833, 8832)].">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4">
Lord Durham granted a general amnesty to the rebels, with only twenty-four exceptions. Eight of the men excepted were political prisoners who had been prominent in the revolt and had confessed their guilt; the remaining sixteen were rebel leaders who had fled from the country. Durham gave orders that the eight prisoners should be transported to the Bermudas during the queen's pleasure, while the sixteen refugees were forbidden to return to Canada under penalty of death [Data: Sources (429, 430)].
</data>
    </node>
