ENTITY: BEAUHARNOIS
Description: Beauharnois was a village in Lower Canada that became a significant flashpoint during the Patriotes' Rebellion of 1837–1838. It served as a gathering point for large bodies of insurgents, primarily disaffected habitants (French-Canadian farmers) who assembled there in substantial numbers to protest colonial authority and support the Patriote cause. These gatherings were part of the broader uprising against British rule and the appointed legislative council. The village was the site of a decisive confrontation. Loyalist forces, acting on behalf of the colonial government, moved against the assembled insurgents and successfully drove them out. The conflict culminated in the defeat of the Patriotes at Beauharnois, marking a key military setback for the rebellion. Following the expulsion of the insurgents, the village itself was burned, a punitive measure that underscored the severity of the crackdown and the destruction wrought by the conflict. In summary, Beauharnois was a central staging ground for the rebellion, where large numbers of disaffected habitants gathered, only to be routed by loyalist troops. The village's burning and the Patriote defeat there highlighted the collapse of the uprising in that region and the harsh consequences faced by those who participated.
