    <node id="QUIBILIER">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">QUIBILIER was a priest who played a key role in advising Louis-Joseph Papineau, a prominent political leader in 19th-century Quebec. According to the available descriptions, Quibilier served as a spiritual and practical counselor to Papineau during a period of political unrest. Specifically, he advised Papineau to leave Montreal in order to avoid further disturbance, likely referring to the escalating tensions and potential violence surrounding the Rebellions of 1837–1838 in Lower Canada. Quibilier’s counsel was delivered in a personal capacity, as he personally waited on Papineau to deliver this advice. His role appears to have been that of a trusted confidant who recognized the imminent danger facing Papineau and urged him to seek safety away from the city. The descriptions are consistent and non-contradictory, both emphasizing Quibilier’s priestly status and his direct, urgent recommendation for Papineau’s departure. While the exact nature of their relationship beyond this moment is not detailed, Quibilier’s actions suggest he was a figure of moral authority and practical prudence, concerned with the well-being of Papineau amid political turmoil. His intervention likely contributed to Papineau’s eventual flight from Montreal, which was a significant event in the lead-up to the rebellion. Thus, Quibilier is remembered as a priest who, through his timely advice, played a minor but notable part in the historical narrative of Papineau’s life and the broader political crisis of the era.
Relationships: None specified in the data.</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
