    <node id="ANDREW STUART">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Andrew Stuart was a prominent lawyer and politician in early 19th-century Lower Canada, best known for his role as a member of the Patriote party. However, he distinguished himself from the party’s more radical leadership by taking a firm stance against the Ninety-Two Resolutions, a set of demands for greater colonial autonomy and democratic reform championed by Louis-Joseph Papineau. Stuart voted against these resolutions, thereby breaking away from Papineau’s faction. Politically, he aligned himself with John Neilson, a moderate reformer who also opposed Papineau’s increasingly confrontational approach. As an able lawyer, Stuart brought legal rigor and pragmatic reasoning to his political work, favoring constitutional and measured opposition over revolutionary rhetoric. His opposition to the Ninety-Two Resolutions placed him in a minority within the Patriote movement, but it underscored his commitment to a more conservative, legalistic path toward reform. Ultimately, Andrew Stuart is remembered as a principled moderate who prioritized stability and legal process over radical change, even at the cost of alienating the dominant wing of his own party.
Relationships: None listed</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
