    <node id="CHARTIER DE LOTBINIERE">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">CHARTIER DE LOTBINIERE was a French-Canadian political figure active during the period of British colonial rule in Canada. He is primarily remembered for his staunch advocacy for the official use of the French language in legislative and governmental affairs. In his arguments, he referenced the demonstrated loyalty of French-Canadians to the British Crown, specifically citing their conduct during the American invasion of 1775, when French-Canadian militias and civilians largely refused to support the American revolutionaries and instead remained loyal to Britain. This historical reference was used by Lotbiniere to justify the continued recognition and protection of French as a legitimate and deserving language within the colonial administration. His position underscored a broader French-Canadian demand for linguistic and cultural rights, framed not as a challenge to British authority but as a reward for past fidelity. Lotbiniere's arguments were part of larger debates over language policy, representation, and the status of the French-Canadian population in the British Empire. His emphasis on the 1775 loyalty served as a powerful rhetorical tool, linking the community's historical sacrifices to contemporary political claims. Overall, Chartier de Lotbiniere stands as a notable advocate for French-Canadian identity and linguistic rights within a British-dominated political system.
Relationships: None listed in the data tables</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
