      <data key="d1">Pierre Bédard was a prominent French-Canadian politician, journalist, and mathematician in Lower Canada. He served as a member of the House of Assembly and was a conspicuous figure on the staff of *Le Canadien*, a newspaper that served as a staunch defender of French-Canadian rights. As one of the proprietors of that publication, he used it as a platform to advocate for his community’s interests. In 1810, he was arrested by Governor James Henry Craig for his political activities. Bédard refused to accept release without a trial, insisting on due process. He was eventually ejected from prison, and later, under Governor Sir George Prevost, he was appointed a judge. He was also the father of Elzear Bédard, who became a political figure in Lower Canada. Notably, Bédard questioned the conventional idea that loyalty to the Crown depended on language, arguing that political allegiance could be separate from linguistic identity. His career reflected a deep commitment to constitutional rights and the protection of French-Canadian political and cultural interests within the British colonial system.
Relationships: None listed</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
