    <node id="LORD GODERICH">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">LORD GODERICH was a British official who served as Colonial Secretary in 1830. During his tenure, he proposed a compromise on revenue control, specifically addressing the financial disputes between the colonial government of Lower Canada and the elected assembly. His 1830 financial proposals were met with opposition from Louis-Joseph Papineau, the leader of the reformist Parti Canadien. Papineau rejected Goderich's compromise, viewing it as insufficient to address the assembly's demands for full control over colonial revenues. This opposition highlighted Papineau's uncompromising stance on constitutional and fiscal autonomy, as he refused to accept partial concessions. Goderich's proposal, while intended to ease tensions, ultimately failed to bridge the gap between the British colonial administration and the reformist movement, contributing to the growing political conflict in Lower Canada.
Relationships: None listed in the data</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
