    <node id="THE AVAILABLE RECORDS CONFIRM THAT THE UNIT WAS PART OF THE BRITISH FORCES OPPOSING THE PATRIOTES, THE FRENCH-CANADIAN REVOLUTIONARY GROUP THAT LED THE REBELLION [DATA: RELATIONSHIPS (13781)].">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4">
The unit's most clearly documented involvement was in the campaign against the rebel stronghold at St. Charles. Lieutenant-Colonel Wetherall's force, which was dispatched to advance on St. Charles, included a troop of the Montreal Volunteer Cavalry [Data: Relationships (13770)]. This force was part of Sir John Colborne's broader plan to disperse rebel gatherings and arrest their leaders. The plan involved Wetherall's column, which included the cavalry, advancing on St. Charles, while another force under Colonel Charles Gore marched on St. Denis [Data: Sources (422)].

The skirmish at Chambly-Longueuil, described as the "Lexington of the Lower Canada rebellion," occurred on November 16, 1837, when British troopers under Lieutenant Ermatinger were confronted by armed Patriotes under Bonaventure Viger. However, the records do not specify whether the Montreal Volunteer Cavalry was involved in this particular skirmish [Data: Sources (422)].

The data does not provide information on the unit's specific commander, notable individual members, or its formation date. The Montreal Volunteer Cavalry is distinct from the Montreal Volunteer Rifles, which was commanded by Sir John Colborne as part of his column [Data: Relationships (13904)].

---
</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THE FILS DE LA LIBERTE ASSOCIATION WAS FORMED THERE IN AUTUMN 1837, AND IT WAS FROM MONTREAL THAT LOUIS-JOSEPH PAPINEAU WAS ADVISED TO LEAVE AFTER DELIVERING AN ADDRESS. THE CITY ALSO SAW A BY-ELECTION IN 1832 THAT CULMINATED IN A RIOT INVOLVING TROOPS AND FRENCH CANADIANS.">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Papineau delivered an address in Montreal and was advised to leave.</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4">
Montreal was a stronghold of British loyalist activity, where constitutional associations were formed, a volunteer rifle corps was organized to protect British interests, and loyalist meetings were held. It was also the seat of Mgr Lartigue's bishopric. Loyalists fled to Montreal to report rebel activities, and it served as the base for Sir John Colborne. The city housed political prisoners in its prisons and was the location of the state trials in 1838-39.

Several key figures were associated with Montreal. Dr. Wolfred Nelson was imprisoned there after his capture, and later returned to practice his profession in the city. Thomas Storrow Brown, an iron-merchant in Montreal, also returned to business there after being injured during the rioting. Papineau delivered an address in the city before being advised to leave.

Montreal was a focal point for rebel planning, as insurgents planned to advance on and capture the city as part of their invasion strategy, though it was never taken. The unrest also led to looting, as described in a letter from an inhabitant of St Benoit to a friend. Throughout the period of unrest, Montreal experienced repeated rioting and served as a center for both loyalist organization and rebel agitation, making it a critical location in the Lower Canada Rebellions.
**Relationships:**
