    <node id="GOVERNMENT">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">The Government is a central ruling authority referenced across multiple contexts, primarily as the British executive body, but also as the governing power in Tunis and as an authority over colonial and domestic affairs. In Britain, it is depicted as a source of ultimate authority whose direct endorsement would lend significant weight to any mission or undertaking. The Government is also the institution where an individual named Ganymede holds a post, using his position to advocate for reformatory views. Additionally, it is noted that the Government was defeated in a vote, indicating a parliamentary or legislative setback. The narrator's father held the British Government in deep awe, viewing it as essential for maintaining social order. In a colonial context, the Government received forfeited lands and annuities from the Sioux people following an outbreak, acting as the beneficiary of punitive measures. The Government is also criticized for its inconsistent enforcement: it frequently punishes Jews for producing white brandy, yet fails to take effective action to stop the practice altogether. In Tunis, the ruling authority receives accounts from overseers and supplies camels, functioning as an administrative and logistical body. There is also a speculative mention that Spike might have been a member of the Government if he belonged to a high family, suggesting that Government membership was tied to aristocratic status. Overall, the Government appears as a multifaceted executive and administrative entity, wielding authority over legal, colonial, and foreign affairs, while also being subject to political defeats and inconsistent policy enforcement.
