    <node id="BRITISH PARLIAMENT">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">The British Parliament is the supreme legislative body of the United Kingdom, serving as the central institution for national law-making and political governance. Historically, it has played a pivotal role in shaping constitutional and social reforms, including the Reform Bill of 1868, which marked the first formal claim for women’s recognition as citizens within the parliamentary framework. This legislative body also holds authority over matters extending beyond the British Isles, as its sanction is required for specific cases of redress of grievances in Lower Canada, reflecting its imperial reach and constitutional oversight during the colonial era. As such, the British Parliament functions both as the domestic lawmaker for the United Kingdom and as a higher authority in certain colonial legal and political processes, embodying a dual role in governance and reform.
Relationships:
  - Source: BRITISH PARLIAMENT
  - Target: REFORM BILL OF 1868
