ENTITY: ST. PAUL FIRE DEPARTMENT
Description: The St. Paul Fire Department is the municipal firefighting organization serving St. Paul, Minnesota. Its history traces back to the 1850s, when it consisted of several volunteer engine and hook and ladder companies. Over time, it evolved from a volunteer force into a fully paid, professional department. By the period referenced in historical records, the department had grown to include 211 men, 15 steamers, 4 chemical units, 8 hook and ladder companies, and 122 horses, reflecting a significant expansion in capacity and equipment.

Beyond its core firefighting duties, the department also participated in civic events, such as Washington's birthday celebrations, indicating its role as an active community institution. The department has been led by a succession of chiefs, including Wash M. Stees, Chas. H. Williams, J.C.A. Pickett, W.T. Donaldson, J.B. Irvine, J.E. Missen, Luther H. Eddy, B. Rodick, M.B. Farrell, J.C. Prendergast, Bartlett Presley, Frank Brewer, R.O. Strong, John T. Black, Hart N. Cook, and John Jackson. These leaders guided the department through its formative years and its transition into a modern, organized municipal service.
Relationships:
  - Source: ST. PAUL FIRE DEPARTMENT
  - Target: FIREMEN AND FIRES OF PIONEER DAYS
  - Description: The history of the St. Paul Fire Department is described in "Firemen and Fires of Pioneer Days."
  - Source: MINNEHAHA ENGINE COMPANY
  - Target: ST. PAUL FIRE DEPARTMENT
  - Description: Minnehaha Engine Company is part of the St. Paul Fire Department.
  - Source: ST. PAUL FIRE DEPARTMENT
  - Target: HOTEL FIRES
  - Description: The St. Paul Fire Department dealt with a large number of hotel fires.
