    <node id="INTERNET ARCHIVE">
      <data key="d3">5</data>
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Internet Archive is a digital library that played a crucial role in the creation of the e-text described in the provided data. Specifically, the organization supplied the page images of the original book, which were then used as the foundational source material for producing the digital version of the text. These page images served as the direct visual reference, enabling the accurate transcription and formatting of the content into a digital format. As a result, Internet Archive is credited as the organization that made these page images available, thereby facilitating the entire digitization process. In summary, Internet Archive functioned as the primary provider of the visual source material, without which the e-text could not have been generated.
Relationships:
  - Source: ONLINE DISTRIBUTED PROOFREADING TEAM
  - Target: INTERNET ARCHIVE
  - Description: The Online Distributed Proofreading Team (DP) is a volunteer-driven initiative that relies on digital scans of public domain books to produce accurate, freely available e-texts. A key resource in this process is the Internet Archive, a vast digital library that hosts millions of scanned books, documents, and other media. The DP team specifically utilizes page images provided by the Internet Archive as the primary source material for its proofreading workflow. By accessing these high-quality scans, volunteers can compare the original page layout and text against the transcribed digital version, ensuring that errors are caught and corrected with high fidelity. The collaboration between the Online Distributed Proofreading Team and the Internet Archive is essential: the Archive supplies the raw visual data (page images), while the DP team transforms those images into clean, searchable, and reliable electronic texts. This partnership streamlines the digitization process, allowing the DP team to focus on textual accuracy without needing to manage physical book scanning or storage. In essence, the Internet Archive serves as the foundational image repository, and the Online Distributed Proofreading Team acts as the meticulous editorial layer, together producing high-quality e-books for public use.
  - Source: ONLINE DISTRIBUTED PROOFREADING TEAM
  - Target: THE INTERNET ARCHIVE
  - Description: The Online Distributed Proofreading Team used images from The Internet Archive to produce the text
  - Source: INTERNET ARCHIVE
  - Target: PROJECT GUTENBERG
  - Description: Internet Archive provides images that are also available through Project Gutenberg's HTML version.
  - Source: DAVID WIDGER
  - Target: INTERNET ARCHIVE
  - Description: David Widger produced the text from images provided by the Internet Archive
  - Source: INTERNET ARCHIVE
  - Target: AMERICAN LIBRARIES
  - Description: American Libraries is a division of the Internet Archive that provided the images
  - Source: THE DEAN
  - Target: THE INTERNET ARCHIVE
  - Description: The Internet Archive provided scan images of the play, which were used for transcription, indirectly relating to the text containing the Dean.
  - Source: PAUL HAXO
  - Target: INTERNET ARCHIVE
  - Description: Paul Haxo produced page images made available by the Internet Archive</data>
