    <node id="COL. MARSHALL">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Colonel Marshall was an officer in the Seventh Regiment, known for his military leadership during the Sioux conflicts. He notably led a charge of approximately 400 men against the Sioux at the Battle of Wood Lake, successfully routing the enemy in that encounter. In addition to his battlefield service, Marshall was selected as a candidate in a sword-voting contest held at a sanitary fair, a competition in which he was backed by friends working in newspaper offices. His candidacy in this contest was supported by these newspaper connections, highlighting his standing within his community and among his peers.
Relationships:
  - Source: COL. MARSHALL
  - Target: SIOUX
  - Description: Col. Marshall led a charge against the Sioux, routing them in the encounter.
  - Source: COL. MARSHALL
  - Target: INSURRECTION
  - Description: Col. Marshall fought in the battle that was part of the insurrection. Col. Marshall fought in the battle of the insurrection.
  - Source: COL. MARSHALL
  - Target: GEN. POPE
  - Description: Col. Marshall's charge was part of the battle that Gen. Pope reported on.
  - Source: COL. MARSHALL
  - Target: SANITARY COMMISSION
  - Description: Col. Marshall was a candidate in a sword-voting contest to raise funds for the Sanitary Commission
  - Source: WOOD LAKE
  - Target: COL. MARSHALL
  - Description: Col. Marshall led a charge against the Sioux at Wood Lake
  - Source: COL. MARSHALL
  - Target: COL. ULINE
  - Description: Col. Marshall and Col. Uline were rival candidates in a sword-voting contest held at the sanitary fair, a charitable event common during the Civil War era. The contest pitted the two colonels against each other, with attendees casting votes—likely in exchange for donations—to determine the winner. Ultimately, Col. Uline emerged victorious, defeating Col. Marshall in the competition. The event highlighted the competitive spirit of the fair, with both men serving as prominent figures in the contest.</data>
