    <node id="GEN. POPE">
      <data key="d3">1</data>
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">General John Pope, a Union general, is the central figure in this summary, though his role is presented with some contextual nuance. Pope, who commanded the Department of the Northwest during the U.S. government’s campaigns against the Sioux, established his headquarters at the International Hotel during that conflict. In the aftermath of the Battle of Wood Lake, he was the general in command of the forces, and following the battle, he telegraphed the War Department to request instructions regarding the disposition of his troops.

However, the descriptions also reference Pope in connection with the Second Battle of Bull Run, where he is identified as a Union general mentioned in the text, though he was not directly involved in the Battle of Pittsburg Landing (also known as Shiloh). This creates a slight contradiction: while one description places him in the Northwest during the Sioux War, another ties him to the Eastern Theater at Bull Run. These are not mutually exclusive, as Pope served in multiple theaters during the Civil War. He commanded the Army of Virginia at the Second Battle of Bull Run (August 1862), where he suffered a defeat, and later was reassigned to the Department of the Northwest (1862–1865), where he led campaigns against the Sioux in Minnesota and the Dakotas. The Battle of Wood Lake (September 1862) occurred during this later assignment, making his command there consistent with his Northwestern role.

Thus, the coherent summary is that John Pope was a Union general who, after his earlier service in the Civil War’s Eastern Theater (including the Second Battle of Bull Run), was placed in command of the Department of the Northwest. In that capacity, he directed military operations against the Sioux, using the International Hotel as his headquarters. Following the Battle of Wood Lake, he remained in command and sought guidance from the War Department on troop deployment. The mention of Pittsburg Landing is a separate historical event (April 1862) in which Pope was not directly involved, as that battle occurred before his Bull Run command and was primarily associated with Generals Grant and Buell. Therefore, Pope’s career spans both the Civil War’s major battles and the subsequent frontier conflicts, with his role in the Sioux War being his most prominent later assignment.
Relationships: None listed in the provided data.

---</data>
