    <node id="GEN. SIBLEY">
      <data key="d3">1</data>
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">GEN. SIBLEY was a Union general during the U.S.–Dakota War of 1862, commanding military operations against the Sioux (Dakota) in Minnesota. He established his headquarters in the International Hotel during the conflict, using it as a base for planning and directing campaigns. Sibley led a significant force that advanced to Camp Release, where he negotiated the surrender of the Indian camp, effectively ending major hostilities in the region. Following this, he formed a military tribunal to try captured Sioux offenders, a process that resulted in numerous convictions and executions. Earlier in the campaign, Sibley arrived at Fort Ridgely, a key defensive post, and subsequently launched offensive operations against the Sioux, culminating in the Battle of Wood Lake. His leadership combined strategic negotiation with punitive military action, and his actions were pivotal in suppressing the uprising and reshaping federal–tribal relations in the Upper Midwest.
Relationships:
  - Source: GEN. SIBLEY
  - Target: FORT RIDGELY
  - Description: Gen. Sibley arrived at Fort Ridgely and used it as a base for operations

---</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
