    <node id="WITHIN THIS REGIMENT, COMPANY B WAS COMMANDED BY CAPTAIN JOHN S. MARSH. THE REGIMENT, PARTICULARLY COMPANY B, WAS INVOLVED IN A SIGNIFICANT AND DEADLY ENGAGEMENT WHEN IT WAS AMBUSHED BY SIOUX WARRIORS. THIS AMBUSH OCCURRED NEAR THE FERRY CROSSING ON THE MINNESOTA RIVER, WHERE CAPTAIN MARSH AND HIS MEN WERE DISPATCHED TO RELIEVE THE BESIEGED SETTLEMENT OF NEW ULM. THE ATTACK RESULTED IN HEAVY CASUALTIES FOR THE FIFTH REGIMENT, INCLUDING THE MORTAL WOUNDING OF CAPTAIN MARSH HIMSELF. THE UNIT'S ACTIONS AT FORT RIDGELY AND DURING THE AMBUSH WERE PART OF THE BROADER CONFLICT BETWEEN THE U.S. MILITARY AND THE SIOUX, MARKING A CRITICAL AND TRAGIC CHAPTER IN THE REGIMENT'S HISTORY. DESPITE THE LOSSES, THE FIFTH REGIMENT'S PRESENCE AT FORT RIDGELY CONTRIBUTED TO THE DEFENSE OF THE FORT DURING SUBSEQUENT ATTACKS. THE REGIMENT'S EXPERIENCE HIGHLIGHTS THE DANGERS FACED BY FRONTIER SOLDIERS AND THE VOLATILE NATURE OF THE 1862 UPRISING.">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
