ENTITY: ARMY OF THE POTOMAC
Description: The Army of the Potomac was a major Union army during the American Civil War, serving as the principal fighting force in the Eastern Theater. It is referenced in a dispatch by Union General Henry W. Halleck, who compared its demoralization following the First Battle of Bull Run to the state of Ulysses S. Grant’s army at a later point, highlighting the challenges of rebuilding morale after early defeats. The army was initially commanded by Major General George B. McClellan, with Major Marcy serving as his chief of staff, reflecting the organizational structure of the force during its early campaigns. The Army of the Potomac also included specialized units, such as a company of sharpshooters organized by William F. Russel, who served within its ranks. Additionally, the army was the setting for a notable incident involving the Hutchinson family, a well-known singing group, who were expelled from the army due to their outspoken abolitionist sentiments, illustrating the political and social tensions within the Union forces. Later in the war, the army came under the command of General Ulysses S. Grant, who led it through the Overland Campaign and the Siege of Petersburg. After the Confederate surrender, the Army of the Potomac passed in review in a grand victory parade, marking the end of its active service. Throughout its existence, the army played a central role in many of the war’s most significant battles, including Antietam, Gettysburg, and the final campaigns in Virginia. Its evolution from a demoralized force after Bull Run to a victorious army under Grant reflects the broader transformation of the Union military effort. The Army of the Potomac remains one of the most studied and remembered military organizations of the Civil War, symbolizing both the struggles and ultimate triumph of the Union cause.
Relationships: (No relationships listed in the provided data)

