    <node id="HE JOINED ROBERT NELSON'S EXPEDITION, WHICH WAS PART OF THE BROADER PATRIOTE REVOLUTIONARY MOVEMENT AGAINST BRITISH COLONIAL RULE IN LOWER CANADA [DATA: RELATIONSHIPS (14152, 14261); SOURCES (432)]. TOUVREY WAS INVEIGLED INTO JOINING THE EXPEDITION, MEANING HE WAS PERSUADED OR TRICKED INTO PARTICIPATING [DATA: RELATIONSHIPS (14262); SOURCES (432)]. HE JOINED THE EXPEDITION AT ST ALBANS, ON THE WEST SIDE OF LAKE CHAMPLAIN, ALONG WITH ANOTHER FRENCH OFFICER NAMED HINDENLANG [DATA: RELATIONSHIPS (14250, 14261); SOURCES (432)].">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4">
Upon arriving at Napierville, which had been designated as the rebel headquarters, TOUVREY and Hindenlang were presented to the crowd that had gathered there [Data: Entities (9294, 9327); Relationships (14191, 14212); Sources (432)]. Robert Nelson was proclaimed "President of the Republic of Lower Canada" at this gathering, and TOUVREY was presented to the assembled crowd as part of the revolutionary proceedings [Data: Relationships (14191, 14212); Sources (432)]. The presentation of TOUVREY and Hindenlang to the crowd at Napierville was a significant event in the rebellion's narrative, as documented in the historical account [Data: Entities (9294, 9327); Relationships (14191, 14212)].

TOUVREY's involvement in the expedition was part of a larger pattern of Canadian refugees gathering along the American border and planning insurrections in Lower Canada during Lord Durham's regime [Data: Sources (432)]. The expedition that TOUVREY joined was one of several attempts by the Patriotes to overthrow British colonial authority, with Robert Nelson and Dr. Cote having previously crossed the border with an armed force in February 1838 [Data: Sources (432)].

---
</data>
    </node>
