    <node id="MRS. FAWCETT">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">MRS. FAWCETT was a prominent British suffragist and a leading figure in the women’s suffrage movement, acknowledged by women across all classes and political affiliations as their leader in 1914. She was also a key organizer and advocate for the representation of women’s work at international exhibitions. In that capacity, she successfully negotiated funding from the English Commissioners for the Chicago Exhibition, initially receiving £500 to represent women’s work in England, and later requesting and obtaining an additional £500 to mount photographs and drawings for the display. Beyond her exhibition work, Mrs. Fawcett was a close friend and supporter of Dr. Inglis, a pioneering female physician. She wrote about Dr. Inglis’s treatment by the War Office, documenting that Dr. Inglis was refused recognition with “contumely and insult.” Mrs. Fawcett noted that while the War Office refused to acknowledge Dr. Inglis’s medical services, the French and Serbian medical services later accepted her, highlighting the injustice of the British refusal. As a prominent suffragist, Mrs. Fawcett was likely a leader in the National Union of Women’s Suffrage Societies, and she was also the recipient of a letter from Elsie Maud, who served as her chief. Her multifaceted role combined political advocacy, organizational leadership, and personal support for women in medicine, making her a central figure in early 20th-century British feminist and social reform movements.
Relationships:
  - Source: MRS. FAWCETT
  - Target: WOMEN SUFFRAGE SOCIETIES
  - Description: Mrs. Fawcett was acknowledged as the leader of the women's suffrage movement.</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">2</data>
