    <node id="SCIENTIFIC AMERICAN SUPPLEMENT">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Scientific American Supplement was a weekly publication that served as a supplement to the long-established Scientific American magazine, which was founded in 1845. As part of the Scientific American family of publications, this supplement was dedicated to covering a broad range of scientific topics, offering readers in-depth articles and papers on contemporary scientific developments. The publication functioned as a weekly periodical, and in addition to its regular content, it provided a catalogue of scientific papers, making it a valuable resource for those seeking both current research and a curated list of related scholarly works. The supplement published original scientific papers and contributed to the dissemination of scientific knowledge within the broader Scientific American framework.
Relationships:
  - Source: SCIENTIFIC AMERICAN SUPPLEMENT
  - Target: MUNN &amp; CO.
  - Description: Munn &amp; Co. is the publisher of the Scientific American Supplement
  - Source: SCIENTIFIC AMERICAN SUPPLEMENT
  - Target: SCIENTIFIC AMERICAN
  - Description: Scientific American Supplement is a supplementary publication issued in conjunction with the main periodical, Scientific American. The two are published together, and they are offered to subscribers at a combined subscription rate, meaning that the Supplement is not sold separately but as part of a bundled subscription with its parent magazine. As a supplement, it extends the content and coverage of Scientific American, providing additional material to readers who subscribe to both. The relationship is integral: the Supplement exists solely as an adjunct to Scientific American, and the combined offering reflects their shared publication and subscription structure.
  - Source: SCIENTIFIC AMERICAN SUPPLEMENT
