    <node id="DURING THIS PERIOD, QUEBEC WAS A SITE OF POLITICAL TENSION: CONSTITUTIONAL ASSOCIATIONS IN THE CITY EXPRESSED DISSATISFACTION WITH GOVERNOR GOSFORD'S POLICIES, AND AN ENGLISH OFFICER WROTE TO GOSFORD REGARDING THE INFLUENCE OF LOUIS-JOSEPH PAPINEAU, WHOSE ELOQUENCE WAS NOTABLY RECOGNIZED IN THE CITY. IN RESPONSE TO THE PATRIOTE MOVEMENT, LOYALIST MEETINGS WERE HELD IN QUEBEC TO OFFSET PATRIOTE GATHERINGS. DESPITE THESE TENSIONS, QUEBEC REMAINED QUIET DURING THE DISTURBANCES OF 1837, AND THE DISTRICT AS A WHOLE STAYED CALM DURING THE REBELLION, LARGELY BECAUSE FIGURES LIKE BÉDARD AND PARENT SUCCESSFULLY CARRIED PUBLIC OPINION IN THE CAPITAL [DATA: ENTITIES (8840)].">
