    <node id="PARÆTONIUM">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Parætonium is a historical city located at the Canopic mouth of the Nile, situated at the western extremity of Egypt, directly adjoining the region of Libya. In antiquity, the city was formerly known as Ammonia, and it is now referred to as al-Baretoun. Parætonium holds significant cultural and religious associations, particularly with the goddess Isis, as it is referenced in ancient texts as a place dedicated to or connected with her worship. The city's strategic position at the Nile's mouth made it an important hub for trade and cultural exchange between Egypt and Libya, while its religious ties to Isis underscore its role in the broader Egyptian spiritual landscape. Today, the site is recognized under its modern name, al-Baretoun, preserving the legacy of its ancient identity as Parætonium.
Relationships: None found in the provided data.</data>
