**ENTITY: CHARLES OVIDE PERRAULT**
Description: Charles Ovide Perrault was a prominent figure in early 19th-century Canadian politics, known for his roles as a member of the Legislative Assembly for Vaudreuil and as a young, ambitious barrister. He was a devoted and close follower, as well as a trusted confidant, of the influential political leader Louis-Joseph Papineau, aligning himself with the reformist and nationalist movements of the time. Perrault actively contributed to the political discourse of his era by writing letters for the newspaper *La Minerve*, a publication that served as a platform for Patriote ideas. His life was tragically cut short during the Lower Canada Rebellion when he was killed in the engagement at St. Denis, a pivotal battle in the conflict. His death marked him as a martyr for the cause he supported, and he is remembered for his youthful dedication, political activism, and ultimate sacrifice in the pursuit of his beliefs.
Relationships: (none listed in the data)

