    <node id="THESE RESOLUTIONS, KNOWN AS THE RUSSELL RESOLUTIONS, DIRECTLY ADDRESSED THE ONGOING POLITICAL AND FINANCIAL CRISES IN THE CANADIAN COLONIES [DATA: ENTITIES (9026, 9028); RELATIONSHIPS (13574)].">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4">
The resolutions were a direct response to the **Ninety-Two Resolutions**, a list of grievances and demands for democratic reform put forward by the Patriotes in Lower Canada. The Russell Resolutions categorically rejected the demand for an elective Legislative Council and, more provocatively, empowered the governor to pay government costs without the approval of the Assembly [Data: Entities (9028); Relationships (13547, 13550, 13552)].

The resolutions declared that it was inadvisable to make the Legislative Council elective, refused to subject the Executive Council to the responsibility demanded by the Assembly, and denied the Assembly's claim to control the executive through the power of the purse [Data: Entities (9028); Relationships (13547, 13550, 13552)].

The main feature of the resolutions was the clause empowering the governor to pay out of the public revenues, without authorization of the Assembly, the moneys necessary for defraying the cost of government in the province up to April 10, 1837. This was a flat negative to the claim of the Lower-Canadian Assembly to control over the executive government [Data: Sources (418)].
</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WHEN DURHAM'S FAMOUS REPORT ON THE AFFAIRS OF BRITISH NORTH AMERICA WAS DEBATED IN THE HOUSE OF COMMONS, RUSSELL PUBLICLY PRAISED DURHAM'S RECOMMENDATIONS AS &quot;WISE AND STATESMANLIKE&quot; [DATA: ENTITIES (9026); RELATIONSHIPS (14096)].">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4">
Lord John Russell was the only minister who gave Durham loyal support during the attacks on Durham's ordinance regarding the treatment of rebels. While other ministers, including Lord Melbourne, capitulated to the Opposition, Russell rose in the House of Commons and made a thoroughgoing defense of Durham's policy [Data: Sources (430); Relationships (14096)].
</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WHEN DURHAM'S FAMOUS REPORT ON THE AFFAIRS OF BRITISH NORTH AMERICA WAS DEBATED IN THE HOUSE OF COMMONS, RUSSELL PUBLICLY PRAISED DURHAM'S RECOMMENDATIONS AS &quot;WISE AND STATESMANLIKE.&quot; THIS ENDORSEMENT WAS SIGNIFICANT, AS IT LENT CONSIDERABLE POLITICAL WEIGHT TO DURHAM'S PROPOSALS, WHICH ULTIMATELY CALLED FOR THE UNION OF UPPER AND LOWER CANADA AND THE INTRODUCTION OF RESPONSIBLE GOVERNMENT—PRINCIPLES THAT WOULD LATER SHAPE THE DEVELOPMENT OF CANADIAN SELF-GOVERNANCE.">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
