      <data key="d1">The KITHARA is an ancient stringed instrument, closely related to the Hebrew kinnor, with both names supporting the identification of the kinnor as a type of lyre. As a Greek lyre, the kithara features a square base and was held against the breast, used for solo performances as early as 700 B.C. Additionally, it is noted as an Asiatic stringed instrument mentioned by historical authors, indicating its widespread cultural and geographical significance.
Relationships:
  - Source: KITHARA
  - Target: GREEKS
  - Description: The kithara was used by the Greeks for solo performances as early as B.C. 700.
  - Source: KITHARA
  - Target: GREEK LYRE
  - Description: Kithara is a type of Greek lyre.
  - Source: KINNOR
  - Target: KITHARA
  - Description: The word kinnor resembles kithara, denoting a lyre
  - Source: KITHARA
  - Target: LYDIANS
  - Description: The Lydians are credited with the three-stringed kithara adopted by the Greeks.</data>
