    <node id="OSIRIS">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">OSIRIS is an ancient Egyptian deity, central to the mythology of the afterlife and the cycle of death and rebirth. He is the son of Seb (the earth god) and Nut (the sky goddess), and the brother of Aroeris, Horus, Typho (also known as Set), Isis, and Nephthis. Osiris became the King of Egypt and is credited with civilizing the world, bringing law, agriculture, and culture to humanity. He was worshiped at Philo (Philae), a major cult center.

In his role as a god of the afterlife, Osiris sat as king and reigning judge over the souls of the dead in Amenti, the region of the West. It was there that Thoth, the god of wisdom and writing, presented the actions of the deceased to Osiris for judgment. This made Osiris the ultimate arbiter of the dead, determining their fate in the lower regions.

Osiris is also known for his tragic narrative: he was killed by his brother Typho (Set), who dismembered him. His wife and sister, Isis, mourned him and, through her rites and magic, restored him to life, making him the first resurrected being and the lord of the underworld. This story is central to Egyptian religious practice, and Osiris is closely associated with the worship of Isis, who is both his sister and his consort. He is referenced in the Metamorphoses, where his connection to Isis's rites is highlighted.

In artistic and symbolic representations, Osiris is often depicted as a mummified king, but he is also associated with the Maya god Chaacmol and is represented as a leopard with an eye above, or wearing a leopard skin. This imagery underscores his power and his connection to the wild and the sacred.

Osiris's influence extends beyond Egypt, as his cult and mythology were widely adopted and adapted in the Greco-Roman world. His role as a dying and resurrecting god, judge of the dead, and civilizer of humanity makes him one of the most important and enduring figures in ancient religion. His worship, particularly at Philae, continued for centuries, and his mythology remains a cornerstone of our understanding of Egyptian beliefs about death, judgment, and the afterlife.
