    <node id="MAZAGRAN">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">MAZAGRAN is a town located on the Atlantic coast of Morocco, historically significant as a coastal settlement. It is described as an ancient and strong castle situated on a peninsula, which was rebuilt by the Portuguese in 1506 under the name Castillo Real. This fortress served as the last Portuguese stronghold in Morocco before being abandoned in 1769. The town featured a small port and maintained trade connections with Rabat. The population of Mazagran is disputed in historical sources, with estimates ranging from as few as 100 to as many as 2,000 inhabitants. Despite its modest size, the town's strategic coastal position and its role in Portuguese colonial history make it a notable location in the region.
