    <node id="THIS CONFLICT, ALSO KNOWN AS THE SECOND ANGLO-MARATHA WAR, WAS A SIGNIFICANT CAMPAIGN FOUGHT BETWEEN THE BRITISH EAST INDIA COMPANY AND THE MARATHA EMPIRE. INGLIS’S INVOLVEMENT IS CONSISTENTLY RECORDED ACROSS MULTIPLE SOURCES, WHICH STATE THAT HE &quot;WENT THROUGH&quot; THE MAHRATTA WAR IN 1803 WHILE STATIONED IN INDIA. THE DESCRIPTIONS PROVIDED ARE NOT CONTRADICTORY BUT RATHER REITERATE THE SAME FACT: THAT INGLIS WAS PRESENT AND ACTIVELY ENGAGED IN THIS MILITARY CAMPAIGN DURING THAT SPECIFIC YEAR. HIS PARTICIPATION PLACES HIM WITHIN THE BROADER CONTEXT OF BRITISH COLONIAL EXPANSION IN THE INDIAN SUBCONTINENT, WHERE THE MAHRATTA WAR WAS A PIVOTAL EVENT THAT SOLIDIFIED BRITISH DOMINANCE IN CENTRAL AND WESTERN INDIA. THE WAR INVOLVED KEY BATTLES SUCH AS ASSAYE, ARGAON, AND LASWARI, AND INGLIS’S SERVICE WOULD HAVE BEEN PART OF THE LARGER BRITISH EFFORT UNDER COMMANDERS LIKE GENERAL ARTHUR WELLESLEY (LATER THE DUKE OF WELLINGTON). WHILE THE AVAILABLE DESCRIPTIONS DO NOT SPECIFY HIS RANK, REGIMENT, OR SPECIFIC ACTIONS DURING THE WAR, THEY CONFIRM HIS DIRECT INVOLVEMENT IN THE 1803 CAMPAIGN. THUS, DAVID INGLIS IS HISTORICALLY RECOGNIZED AS A PARTICIPANT IN THE MAHRATTA WAR OF 1803, A CONFLICT THAT SHAPED THE POLITICAL LANDSCAPE OF INDIA AND CONTRIBUTED TO THE RISE OF BRITISH POWER IN THE REGION. HIS EXPERIENCE IN THIS WAR LIKELY FORMED PART OF HIS BROADER MILITARY CAREER IN INDIA, THOUGH FURTHER DETAILS ABOUT HIS LIFE REMAIN UNSPECIFIED IN THE GIVEN DATA. OVERALL, THE CONSISTENT MENTION OF HIS PARTICIPATION UNDERSCORES HIS ROLE AS A SOLDIER OR OFFICER ENGAGED IN ONE OF THE MOST CONSEQUENTIAL MILITARY ENGAGEMENTS OF THE EARLY 19TH CENTURY IN SOUTH ASIA.">
