    <node id="SCOTTISH FEDERATION">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">The Scottish Federation, formally known as the Scottish Federation of Suffrage Societies, served as a regional branch of the National Union of Women’s Suffrage Societies. Under its governance, the Federation played a pivotal role in the establishment and early organization of the Scottish Women’s Hospital for Foreign Service. In October 1914, the Federation formally adopted the scheme for these hospitals, thereby overseeing their initial creation, strategic decisions, and operational framework. Dr. Elsie Inglis, a prominent physician and suffragist, acted as the Federation’s Honorary Secretary, through which she channeled her organizational and medical expertise into the wartime hospital initiative. The Federation’s work combined its suffrage advocacy with humanitarian medical relief, reflecting a broader commitment to women’s public service during World War I.
Relationships:
  - Source: FEDERATION OF SCOTTISH SUFFRAGE SOCIETIES
  - Target: DR. INGLIS
  - Description: Dr. Inglis was Honorary Secretary to the Scottish Federation and owed much to her organizational gift.</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
