    <node id="PHOEBUS">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Phoebus, another name for the Greek and Roman god Apollo, is a multifaceted deity referenced across various contexts as a patron of the arts, music, poetry, prophecy, and the sun. As the sun god, Phoebus is contrasted with passionate, human affection—he would not kiss Diana in such a manner—highlighting his divine, distant nature. He is also the god of music and poetry, serving as the patron of the speaker’s poetic abilities, and is closely associated with the Muses, who inspire creative work. In this role, Phoebus is depicted as a figure whose lyre, a symbol of his artistic power, might be threatened by Cupid, or who could be armed with a spear, showing his dual nature as both a creative and martial deity.

Beyond the arts, Phoebus is the god of prophecy and augury, invoked to be propitious to augurs—those who interpret omens and divine will. His prophetic aspect is further illustrated in a mythological example where his slave priestess, Chryseis, was loved by the General from Mycenae (Agamemnon), a story that underscores his involvement in human affairs and the consequences of defying his will. Additionally, Phoebus is referenced as a deity to whom a Lesbian damsel (likely the poet Sappho) may dedicate her lyre, indicating his role as a divine recipient of artistic devotion.

Overall, Phoebus (Apollo) embodies a complex blend of solar radiance, artistic inspiration, prophetic insight, and divine authority. He is invoked as a source of poetic and musical talent, a guardian of prophecy, and a figure whose actions and attributes shape both mortal creativity and mythological narratives. The descriptions consistently identify Phoebus as Apollo, emphasizing his central role in Greek and Roman religion and literature as a god who bridges the realms of light, art, and foresight.  
**Relationships:**  
  - Source: PHOEBUS  
  - Target: CHRYSEIS  
  - Description: Phoebus (Apollo) is the god whose priestess Chryseis was, and she was loved by the General from Mycenae.

---</data>
