    <node id="BACCHUS">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">BACCHUS is a prominent deity in classical mythology, most widely recognized as the Roman god of wine, revelry, and youth. He is also associated with persuasion, love, and the night, and is frequently compared to the figure of Love (Eros/Cupid) in literary texts. As the god of wine, Bacchus is credited with subduing the Gangetic land, and his legendary Indian triumphs extended as far as the river Ganges, marking him as a conqueror of vast territories. In his military campaigns, particularly his Indian expedition, Bacchus and his troops famously used vine and ivy to conceal their spears, blending nature with warfare.

Bacchus is also deeply connected to themes of intoxication and boldness. He is described as being without fear and highly persuasive, traits that are mirrored in the speaker's own state of drunkenness and newfound courage in certain texts. His influence over wine and revelry makes him a natural companion to love and nighttime festivities, where his persuasive power holds sway.

In addition to his martial and festive aspects, Bacchus has a unique mythological origin. After the death of his mother, Semele, his father (Zeus/Jupiter) performed maternal duties for him, carrying the unborn Bacchus to term in his own thigh. This unusual birth story underscores his divine nature and his connection to both life and rebirth.

Bacchus is also honored alongside Ceres, the goddess of agriculture, by husbandmen and farmers, reflecting his role in the fertility of the land and the joy of harvest. Together, they represent the dual blessings of grain and wine, essential to human sustenance and celebration.

Overall, Bacchus embodies a complex blend of conquest, persuasion, intoxication, and youthful vitality. He is a god who brings both ecstasy and courage, who triumphs over distant lands, and who is intimately tied to the pleasures and dangers of wine. His presence in mythology bridges the realms of nature, war, love, and festivity, making him a multifaceted figure whose influence extends from the battlefield to the banquet hall.
Relationships:
  - Source: BACCHUS
  - Target: Love (Eros/Cupid)
  - Description: Bacchus is frequently compared to the figure of Love (Eros/Cupid) in literary texts
  - Source: BACCHUS
  - Target: Semele (mother)
  - Description: After the death of his mother Semele, his father (Zeus/Jupiter) carried the unborn Bacchus to term in his own thigh
  - Source: BACCHUS
  - Target: Zeus/Jupiter (father)
  - Description: Zeus/Jupiter performed maternal duties for Bacchus, carrying him to term in his own thigh
  - Source: BACCHUS
  - Target: Ceres
  - Description: Bacchus is honored alongside Ceres, the goddess of agriculture, by husbandmen and farmers

---</data>
