    <node id="SERPENT">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">SERPENT is a multifaceted and deeply symbolic entity, primarily revered as the protecting spirit of the Maya gods and the personal totem of their father, Can. In this role, the serpent stands as a formidable enemy of the Sun, a cosmic opposition that spans the mythologies of both Mayab and Egypt. As Can’s totem, it is intrinsically linked to his lineage and divine authority, with the family’s worship evolving over time to represent the serpent as a winged creature, known as Kukulcan, in later religious practices.

Beyond its protective and familial associations, the serpent is also depicted as a sluggish, earthbound creature that coils around the altars of the goddess Isis, a reference that ties it to Egyptian ritual and the veneration of the divine feminine. This imagery reinforces its role as a sacred guardian, present in the very architecture of worship. The serpent’s symbolism extends to the physical representation of the gods themselves, appearing in the jaws of a bust—likely a depiction of a deity—and serving as a prominent emblem of Oannes, the Babylonian fish-god, further cementing its cross-cultural significance.

While the descriptions present the serpent in various contexts—as a cosmic adversary, a domestic totem, a ritual ornament, and a divine emblem—they are not contradictory but rather complementary facets of a single, powerful archetype. The serpent is consistently portrayed as a conduit of divine power, a protector of the gods and their chosen representatives, and a symbol of wisdom and eternity. Its dual nature—both sluggish and winged, earthly and celestial—highlights its ability to bridge the mortal and the divine. Ultimately, the SERPENT emerges as a unifying symbol across Mesoamerican, Egyptian, and Mesopotamian traditions, embodying the protective, generative, and sometimes adversarial forces of the cosmos, all while remaining the enduring totem of Can and his divine progeny.
Relationships: None listed in the provided data.</data>
