    <node id="ODIN">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">ODIN is a central deity in Norse mythology, revered as a powerful and multifaceted god. He is described as a Norse god who holds a throne in the skies, a position that connects him to water-spirits and rain deities, suggesting his dominion over atmospheric and aquatic forces. In this role, he is also known as Nikarr, a name that underscores his rulership over the sea, further cementing his association with both celestial and marine realms.

Odin was a principal figure in the pre-Christian religion of the early English ancestors, who worshipped him as a core part of their spiritual beliefs. This worship extended beyond Scandinavia, indicating his widespread influence across Germanic and Anglo-Saxon cultures. As a Scandinavian god, he is credited as the originator of magic songs, a role that highlights his mastery over runes, wisdom, and esoteric knowledge, making him a patron of poets, shamans, and seekers of hidden truths.

While the descriptions emphasize his connections to water, sky, and magic, they collectively portray Odin as a sovereign deity whose authority spans the natural and supernatural worlds. His throne in the skies symbolizes his overarching power, while his link to water-spirits and rain deities ties him to life-giving and destructive forces of nature. The name Nikarr, meaning "sea-ruler," reinforces his maritime sovereignty, and his role as the originator of magic songs establishes him as a divine source of arcane wisdom.

These attributes are not contradictory but complementary, painting Odin as a complex god who bridges the heavens, the sea, and the realm of human knowledge. His worship among early English ancestors and his Scandinavian origins show a shared cultural reverence, with his influence persisting in folklore and later literary traditions. In summary, Odin is a supreme Norse deity, a sky-and-sea ruler, a master of magic, and a foundational figure in the religious heritage of Northern European peoples, whose legacy endures as a symbol of wisdom, power, and the mysteries of the natural world.
Relationships:
  - Source: ODIN
  - Target: Water-spirits and rain deities
  - Description: Odin's throne in the skies connects him to water-spirits and rain deities, suggesting his dominion over atmospheric and aquatic forces
  - Source: ODIN
  - Target: Nikarr (name/identity)
  - Description: Odin is also known as Nikarr, a name that underscores his rulership over the sea

---</data>
