    <node id="THOTH">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">THOTH is an ancient Egyptian deity whose roles and attributes are extensively documented across various traditions. He is primarily recognized as the preceptor or teacher of the goddess Isis, imparting wisdom and sacred knowledge to her. Thoth is credited with the invention of letters, arithmetic (or reckoning), geometry, and astronomy, positioning him as a divine patron of intellectual pursuits and the written word. In artistic and symbolic representations, Thoth is frequently depicted as a baboon, specifically the cynocephalus species, though he is also commonly shown as an ibis-headed man in other contexts. His duties extend into the afterlife, where he serves as the scribe in the lower regions (the underworld). In this capacity, he meticulously records the deeds of the deceased and presents these accounts to the god Osiris during the judgment of the soul, playing a crucial role in the funerary and judgment processes.

The name "Thoth" carries significant linguistic and symbolic weight. In hieroglyphs, his name is written as "Nukta," which bears a notable connection to the Maya language, where "Nuctah" means "to understand." This etymological link underscores Thoth's association with comprehension and intellectual clarity. Furthermore, the name "Thoth" itself corresponds to the Maya word "thot," which means "to scatter flowers," a metaphor for the dissemination of knowledge and the flourishing of wisdom. Together, these linguistic connections reinforce Thoth's identity as a deity of understanding, communication, and the generous distribution of learning.

In summary, Thoth is a multifaceted ancient Egyptian god who embodies wisdom, writing, science, and cosmic order. He is the divine teacher of Isis, the inventor of foundational disciplines such as writing, mathematics, geometry, and astronomy, and the celestial recorder of human deeds in the afterlife. His iconography as a baboon highlights his role as a scribe and observer, while his name's resonance with Maya terms for understanding and scattering flowers symbolizes the active and generative nature of knowledge. Thoth stands as a central figure in Egyptian mythology, bridging the mortal and divine realms through his mastery of language, measurement, and judgment.
Relationships: None listed in the provided data.</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">3</data>
