    <node id="APOLLO">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Apollo is a prominent deity in both Greek and Roman mythology, known as the son of Zeus and associated with a wide range of domains including music, poetry, prophecy, archery, and athletic excellence. He is celebrated for his skill in boxing, as noted in the context of ancient athletic games, and is also linked to the flexible bow, symbolizing his mastery of archery. Apollo is frequently depicted with the lyre, an instrument central to his identity as the god of music and poetic inspiration. In early Christian art, he appears touching the lyre, a symbolic representation of Christ, and is also shown in similar contexts as a figure of divine harmony.

Apollo's mythological roles extend to his familial connections: he is the father of Linus and Orpheus, both figures associated with music and lament. Through these relationships, Apollo is tied to poetic inspiration and the expression of death laments, underscoring his influence over both creative and mournful arts. He is also described as a god who pierced Tityus with darts, demonstrating his role as an avenger or punisher in myth. Additionally, his temple on the Palatine Hill was adorned with statues of Danaus and his daughters, linking him to significant mythological narratives and Roman cultural heritage.

In poetic and artistic traditions, Apollo is associated with the Castalian stream, from which he supplies cups, and is connected to the laurel and myrtle, plants sacred to him. He is also linked to the Lesbian damsel and the lyre, further emphasizing his patronage of music and poetry. Across various texts, Apollo is consistently portrayed as a multifaceted god whose influence spans prophecy, the arts, and athletic prowess, making him one of the most versatile and revered figures in classical mythology. His depictions in both Greek and Roman contexts, as well as in early Christian art, highlight his enduring cultural significance and adaptability across different religious and artistic traditions.
Relationships:
  - Source: APOLLO
  - Target: Zeus (father)
  - Description: Apollo is the son of Zeus
  - Source: APOLLO
  - Target: Linus (son)
  - Description: Apollo is the father of Linus, a figure associated with music and lament
  - Source: APOLLO
  - Target: Orpheus (son)
  - Description: Apollo is the father of Orpheus, a figure associated with music and lament
  - Source: APOLLO
  - Target: Tityus
  - Description: Apollo pierced Tityus with darts, demonstrating his role as an avenger or punisher in myth
  - Source: APOLLO
  - Target: Danaus and his daughters
  - Description: Apollo's temple on the Palatine Hill was adorned with statues of Danaus and his daughters

---</data>
