    <node id="BRAHMA">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">BRAHMA is a significant deity in Hinduism, primarily recognized within the context of the Rig Veda and broader Hindu cosmology. He is a Hindu god, often depicted as a vigorous man with four heads, and is frequently shown beating a small drum, symbolizing the rhythmic creation and passage of time. In his role as a creator deity, Brahma is closely associated with Maya, a concept that represents the illusory and creative power of the universe. Maya is described as a feminine energy and a productive virtue, working in tandem with Brahma during the act of creation. This association highlights Brahma's function not only as a physical creator but also as a conduit for the divine, dynamic force that shapes reality. Together, Brahma and Maya embody the generative principle, where Brahma provides the conscious will and Maya supplies the material and energetic substance, making creation possible. Thus, Brahma stands as a central figure in Hindu theology, bridging the abstract power of Maya with the tangible, manifested world.
Relationships:
  - Source: BRAHMA
  - Target: Maya
  - Description: Brahma is closely associated with Maya, a concept representing the illusory and creative power of the universe; Maya is described as a feminine energy and a productive virtue working in tandem with Brahma during creation

---</data>
