    <node id="CAN">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">CAN is a foundational figure in Mayab mythology, regarded as the founder of the principal cities of Mayab, including Uxmal and Chichen. He is described as an ancient Mayan ancestor or leader who, along with his ancestors, adopted the head of the mastodon as a symbol of deity, indicating that they were contemporary with the animal. His name, meaning "serpent," is written on monuments in both Uxmal and Chichen, and he is closely associated with the serpent symbol throughout the texts.

CAN is the husband of COZ and the father of several important figures: CAY, AAK, CHAACMOL, MOO, and NICTE. He is also identified as the father of Kinich-kakmo, a deity in Mayab mythology. A mound and turret are dedicated to him at Uxmal, underscoring his significance in the region's religious and architectural history.

As the founder of the family that established both Uxmal and Chichen, CAN's legacy extends beyond his immediate lineage. His children's history is said to be preserved in Egypt and Hindostan, and his royal family reached apotheosis, being worshiped as gods. This suggests that CAN's influence and mythological importance transcended the Mayab region, linking his dynasty to broader ancient traditions.

The descriptions consistently portray CAN as a patriarchal founder, a cultural hero, and a divine ancestor. His association with the serpent and the mastodon's head highlights his role as a bridge between the natural world and the divine, as well as a symbol of continuity between ancient times and the later Mayan civilization. While some texts emphasize his role as a city founder, others focus on his familial and divine connections, but all agree on his central place in Mayab mythology and history.
Relationships: None listed in the provided data.</data>
