    <node id="DUKE OF WELLINGTON">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Duke of Wellington was a prominent British military leader and politician, best known for his role as a commander during the Napoleonic Wars and his subsequent political career. As a military figure, he achieved lasting fame for his victory at the Battle of Waterloo. In politics, he served as a leading member of the Tory party and held high office, including as Prime Minister of the United Kingdom. He was generally regarded as a capable and effective ruler, though his reputation was complicated by his support for Catholic emancipation, a policy that proved controversial among his contemporaries and led to criticism from some factions. Later in his political life, Wellington was noted for his involvement in parliamentary affairs, particularly his support for Henry Brougham's attack on Lord Durham, demonstrating his continued influence within Tory circles. Despite the mixed reactions to his policies, Wellington remained a central and respected figure in British public life, bridging military and political spheres.
Relationships:
  - Source: LORD BROUGHAM
  - Target: DUKE OF WELLINGTON
  - Description: Duke of Wellington supported Lord Brougham's attack on Durham</data>
