    <node id="HE WAS KNOWN FOR HIS CONVIVIAL MANNERS AND POLICY OF CONCILIATION, ACTIVELY SEEKING TO REDRESS GRIEVANCES BY OFFERING CONCESSIONS TO FRENCH CANADIANS AND ATTEMPTING TO RECONCILE THE FRENCH AND ENGLISH POPULATIONS [DATA: ENTITIES (9008, 9014)].">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4">
His approach included entertaining lavishly and even inducing Papineau to accept his hospitality, despite warnings that Papineau was irreconcilable. One English officer in Quebec wrote to Gosford that Papineau would "transmute every public concession into a demand for more" and that his "disordered moral palate, beneath the blandest smile and the softest language, will turn your Burgundy into vinegar" [Data: Sources (417)].

In his speech opening the legislature in autumn 1835, Gosford announced his determination to redress every grievance, promised no discrimination against French Canadians in appointments, expressed that executive councillors should not sit in the legislature, guaranteed the French use of their native tongue, and offered concessions on financial control. He made an earnest plea for reconciliation between the French and English races [Data: Sources (417)].

However, his policy was undermined by the indiscreet action of Sir Francis Bond Head, the newly appointed lieutenant-governor of Upper Canada, who communicated his instructions from the Colonial Office to the Upper Canada legislature. This revealed that the British government did not contemplate real constitutional changes, particularly not an elective Legislative Council. This information, brought to Papineau's attention by Marshall Spring Bidwell, confirmed Papineau's belief that justice could not be hoped for [Data: Sources (417)].
</data>
    </node>
    <node id="A MANIFESTO OF YOUNGER PATRIOTE MEMBERS, ISSUED ON OCTOBER 1, 1837, SPOKE OF &quot;PROUD DESIGNS, WHICH IN OUR DAY MUST EMANCIPATE OUR BELOVED COUNTRY FROM ALL HUMAN AUTHORITY EXCEPT THAT OF THE BOLD DEMOCRACY RESIDING WITHIN ITS BOSOM.&quot; VOLUNTEER COMPANIES OF ARMED PATRIOTES SPRANG UP ALL OVER THE COUNTRY, LED AND ORGANIZED BY MILITIA OFFICERS WHO HAD BEEN DISMISSED FOR SEDITIOUS UTTERANCES [DATA: SOURCES (420)].">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4">
A great meeting was held on October 23 at St Charles on the Richelieu, the largest and most imposing of all the meetings thus far. Five to six thousand people attended, representing all the counties about the Richelieu. Dr. Wolfred Nelson was in the chair, but Papineau was the central figure. A company of armed men saluted every resolution with a volley, and a wooden pillar with a cap of liberty was erected and dedicated to Papineau. Each person present swore to be faithful to the cause of their country, "to conquer or die for her" [Data: Sources (420)].

Papineau deprecated any recourse to arms and advised his hearers merely to boycott English goods. However, some of his lieutenants used dangerous language, with Wolfred Nelson crying, "The time has come to melt our spoons into bullets." Papineau's exact attitude during these months was difficult to determine—he had completely lost faith in British justice and earnestly desired emancipation from British rule, but could not commit himself to armed rebellion [Data: Sources (420)].
</data>
    </node>
