    <node id="KNOWN FOR HIS CONVIVIAL MANNERS AND A POLICY OF CONCILIATION, HE ACTIVELY SOUGHT TO REDRESS GRIEVANCES BY OFFERING CONCESSIONS TO FRENCH CANADIANS AND ATTEMPTING TO RECONCILE THE FRENCH AND ENGLISH POPULATIONS. DESPITE THESE EFFORTS, HIS APPROACH ULTIMATELY FAILED. IN A LETTER TO THE COLONIAL SECRETARY DATED SEPTEMBER 2, 1837, GOSFORD STATED THAT CONCILIATION HAD FAILED AND SUGGESTED THE SUSPENSION OF THE CONSTITUTION. HE WAS ALSO ASKED BY LOUIS-HIPPOLYTE LAFONTAINE TO CALL THE LEGISLATURE BUT REFUSED TO DO SO. GOSFORD REMAINED IN CANADA DURING THE REBELLION ITSELF, ONLY RETURNING HOME AT THE END OF FEBRUARY 1838, AFTER HIS TENURE HAD EFFECTIVELY ENDED. HIS GOVERNORSHIP IS THUS MARKED BY A GENUINE BUT UNSUCCESSFUL ATTEMPT AT PEACEFUL REFORM, FOLLOWED BY THE COLLAPSE OF HIS CONCILIATORY STRATEGY AND THE ONSET OF ARMED CONFLICT.">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
