</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THE GROUP WAS COMPOSED LARGELY OF FARMERS AND OTHER LOCAL SUPPORTERS, THOUGH IT ALSO INCLUDED NOTABLE FIGURES SUCH AS CHENIER AND GIROD, WHO GATHERED AT ST EUSTACHE, AND MEMBERS LIKE PERRAULT AND BROWN. THE PATRIOTES FOUGHT AGAINST BRITISH AUTHORITY IN SEVERAL ENGAGEMENTS, INCLUDING THE DEFENSE OF ST DENIS, WHERE THEY INITIALLY RESISTED BRITISH FORCES. DESPITE THEIR EFFORTS, THEY WERE ULTIMATELY DEFEATED. IN THE AFTERMATH, SOME PATRIOTES CARED FOR WOUNDED BRITISH SOLDIERS, WHILE OTHERS FLED, WITH SOME BEING PROMISED ARMS AND SUPPLIES FROM THE UNITED STATES.">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">The Patriotes were led primarily by Louis-Joseph Papineau.</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4">
The movement was both political and revolutionary, advocating for independence and resistance against British rule. Although the rebellions failed, the Patriotes are remembered as a key force in the struggle for political reform and French-Canadian rights in Lower Canada.
