    <node id="HE WAS A CLOSE ASSOCIATE OF LOUIS-JOSEPH PAPINEAU, A PROMINENT POLITICAL FIGURE AND LEADER OF THE REBELLION. O'CALLAGHAN WAS PRESENT AT THE BATTLE OF ST. DENIS, ONE OF THE KEY EARLY CONFRONTATIONS OF THE UPRISING, BUT HE LEFT THE BATTLEFIELD ALONGSIDE PAPINEAU BEFORE THE ENGAGEMENT CONCLUDED. FOLLOWING THE DEFEAT AT ST. CHARLES, ANOTHER MAJOR BATTLE OF THE REBELLION, O'CALLAGHAN FLED TO THE VERMONT BORDER, CROSSING INTO THE UNITED STATES TO ESCAPE ARREST BY BRITISH AUTHORITIES. HE IS CONSISTENTLY DESCRIBED AS A REBEL LEADER WHO FLED ACROSS THE AMERICAN BORDER AFTER THE REBELLION'S FAILURE, WITH MULTIPLE ACCOUNTS CONFIRMING HIS ESCAPE. HIS ACTIONS REFLECT THE BROADER COLLAPSE OF THE REBELLION AND THE SUBSEQUENT EXILE OF MANY PATRIOTE LEADERS. O'CALLAGHAN'S ROLE WAS SIGNIFICANT ENOUGH TO BE NOTED IN HISTORICAL RECORDS, THOUGH HIS SPECIFIC POST-EXILE ACTIVITIES ARE NOT DETAILED IN THE PROVIDED DESCRIPTIONS. HE REMAINS A FIGURE ASSOCIATED WITH THE REVOLUTIONARY EFFORTS IN LOWER CANADA, PARTICULARLY FOR HIS INVOLVEMENT IN THE EARLY BATTLES AND HIS SUBSEQUENT FLIGHT TO SAFETY IN THE UNITED STATES.">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
