    <edge source="PATRIOTES" target="BRITISH AUTHORITIES" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The British authorities opposed the Patriotes in the rebellion</data>
      <data key="d8">BRITISH AUTHORITIES</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATRIOTES" target="MONTREAL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">In 1837, the city of Montreal was a central site of unrest during the Lower Canada Rebellion, directly linked to the Patriote movement. The Patriotes, a reformist and nationalist political group, sparked significant civil disorder in the city, with rioting breaking out in connection to their activities. In response to these rebel actions, a group of Loyalistscolonists who remained loyal to the British Crownfled from the nearby town of St. Eustache to Montreal. Their purpose was to report the rebel activities they had witnessed, seeking to alert authorities and coordinate a counter-response. Thus, Montreal served as both a flashpoint for Patriote-led agitation and a refuge for Loyalists gathering intelligence against the rebellion.</data>
      <data key="d8">MONTREAL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATRIOTES" target="COLONIAL OFFICE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Patriotes dealt with the Colonial Office through deputations</data>
      <data key="d8">COLONIAL OFFICE</data>
    </edge>
