      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The October 1, 1837 manifesto was issued by younger spirits of the Patriote party</data>
      <data key="d8">OCTOBER 1, 1837 MANIFESTO</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATRIOTES" target="RUSSELL, LORD JOHN" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Russell Resolutions cause great agitation among the Patriotes, indicating a political trigger. Russell's resolutions agitate the Patriotes, indicating a political trigger.</data>
      <data key="d8">RUSSELL, LORD JOHN</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATRIOTES" target="RUSSELL RESOLUTIONS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Patriotes, a reformist political movement in Lower Canada, were central to the escalating conflict with the British colonial government in the 1830s. Their primary grievance was the series of legislative measures known as the Russell Resolutions, which were issued by the British Parliament in 1837. These resolutions were a direct and hostile response to the Patriotes' earlier demands, most notably the Ninety-Two Resolutionsa comprehensive list of political and social reforms that the Patriotes had submitted to London. The Russell Resolutions categorically rejected the Ninety-Two Resolutions, refusing to grant the Patriotes any of their key requests, such as responsible government and greater local control over finances. The Patriotes viewed the Russell Resolutions as a profound betrayal and an insult to their cause. They publicly condemned the measures, describing them as a "foul stain" on their political aspirations and on the principles of justice and self-governance. This rejection was not merely a political setback; it was a catalyst that radicalized the Patriotes, pushing them from peaceful petitioning toward open rebellion. The conflict escalated rapidly after the resolutions were announced, as the Patriotes organized protests, boycotts, and eventually armed resistance, leading to the Lower Canada Rebellion of 18371838. In summary, the Russell Resolutions were the British government's firm refusal to accommodate the Patriotes' demands, and this refusal directly inflamed tensions. The Patriotes, in turn, responded with fierce opposition, framing the resolutions as an illegitimate and oppressive act. Their protest against the resolutions marked a turning point, transforming a constitutional struggle into a revolutionary one. The interplay between the two entitiesthe Patriotes' demands and the Russell Resolutions' rejectiondefined the political crisis that ultimately led to armed conflict and the eventual suspension of the colonial constitution. The Patriotes' legacy is thus inseparable from their confrontation with the Russell Resolutions, which they saw as the embodiment of colonial tyranny and a direct assault on their rights.</data>
      <data key="d8">RUSSELL RESOLUTIONS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATRIOTES" target="GLADSTONE, W. E." id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Gladstone supported the Russell Resolutions, which opposed the Patriotes.</data>
      <data key="d8">RUSSELL RESOLUTIONS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATRIOTES" target="STANLEY, LORD" id="0">
