    <edge source="ROBERT NELSON" target="CANADA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Robert Nelson was a Canadian Patriote and a key figure in the struggle for Canadian independence during the 19th century. He is best known for declaring the independence of Canada under a provisional government, a significant act in the context of the Patriote movement. Following the rebellion, Nelson left Canada and relocated to New York, where he continued his political activities in exile. His actions were part of the broader efforts to establish a sovereign Canadian state, distinct from British colonial rule. Nelson's declaration of independence, though not ultimately successful in establishing a lasting government, marked a pivotal moment in Canadian political history, reflecting the aspirations of the Patriotes for self-governance and national autonomy. His life and work are remembered as integral to the early Canadian independence movement.</data>
      <data key="d8">ROBERT NELSON</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ROBERT NELSON" target="REBELLION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Robert Nelson was a prominent leader of the rebellion against British rule, playing a key role in organizing and directing the uprising. Following the failure of the rebellion, he fled across the American border to escape capture and prosecution. His actions as a rebel leader were central to the movement, and his subsequent flight marked the end of his direct involvement in the conflict.</data>
      <data key="d8">ROBERT NELSON</data>
    </edge>
