    <edge source="BEY" target="ARABS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Bey (BEY) holds a position of clear authority over the Arabs (ARABS), as demonstrated through multiple interactions during a military campaign or ceremonial march. He issues orders to the Arab contingent, who in turn show their allegiance by presenting him with gifts of horses. This hierarchical relationship is further reinforced by the Beys ownership of horses and his capacity to administer punishment, indicating his direct control over the Arab group. During the march, the Bey is accompanied by Arab guards, whom he leads and formally salutes, underscoring his role as their commander. The Arabs demonstrate their loyalty and martial skill by performing "playing at powder"a traditional display of gunfirebefore the Bey. This spectacle is repeated as the Arabs gallop ahead of him, firing their guns in a show of respect and celebration. These actions collectively illustrate a dynamic where the Bey commands, receives tribute, and is honored through ceremonial gunfire, while the Arabs serve as his mounted escort and perform feats of horsemanship and marksmanship in his presence.</data>
      <data key="d8">BEY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BEY" target="TUNIS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Bey, a title held by the rulers of the region, is closely associated with the city of Tunis, which served as his primary residence and the source of his water supply. According to the available descriptions, the Bey lived in Tunis and drank water that was specifically brought from that city. This water was not merely local but was transported to him, indicating a deliberate and significant connection between the ruler and Tunis as the origin of his drinking water. The Beys water supply consistently came from Tunis, reinforcing the idea that Tunis was not only his home but also the essential provider of a vital resource. All descriptions agree on this point, with no contradictions: the Bey resided in Tunis, and his water was sourced from there, whether described as being drunk directly, brought to him, or supplied from the city. Thus, the Beys identity and daily life were fundamentally tied to Tunis, both as his place of residence and as the exclusive origin of his water.</data>
      <data key="d8">BEY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BEY" target="R." id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Bey, a title used for a regional ruler or leader, presented R. with several gifts, demonstrating his favor and generosity. Among these offerings was a hobara, a type of bustard bird, which was given as a personal present. Additionally, the Bey sent a fine bay horse to R., indicating a gift of significant value and prestige. Beyond these individual items, the Bey also dispatched live animals to R., which included the aforementioned horse and hobara, as well as other unspecified creatures. The descriptions collectively confirm that the Beys gifts to R. comprised both a specific bird and a horse, along with a broader assortment of live animals, all sent as tokens of esteem. There is no contradiction among the accounts; rather, they complement each other, with the more general statement about live animals encompassing the specific mentions of the hobara and the bay horse. Thus, R. received a varied and notable collection of living gifts from the Bey, reflecting the rulers wealth and the importance of their relationship.</data>
      <data key="d8">BEY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BEY" target="CAPTAIN B." id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Captain B., also referred to as CAPTAIN B., is a figure associated with the Bey, a prominent entity in the context of their shared travels. According to the available descriptions, Captain B. felt a strong sense of protection from the Bey while journeying through various districts. This suggests that the Bey served as a guardian or influential protector during these expeditions, offering safety and security to Captain B. along the way. Additionally, Captain B. was a member of a group that formally paid respects to the Bey and accompanied him during his travels. This indicates that Captain B. held a position of deference or loyalty toward the Bey, and that their relationship was marked by mutual recognition and ceremonial or respectful interaction. The two descriptions together paint a picture of Captain B. as a devoted companion and follower of the Bey, who relied on the Beys protective presence and participated in the formalities of honoring him. There is no contradiction between the statements; rather, they complement each other, showing both the emotional security Captain B. experienced and the social or ritual role he played within the Beys traveling entourage. Thus, Captain B. emerges as a loyal, respectful, and protected associate of the Bey, deeply involved in the latters journeys across the districts.</data>
      <data key="d8">BEY</data>
    </edge>
