    <edge source="MONTREAL" target="REBELLION OF 1837" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">MONTREAL, a city in what is now Quebec, Canada, was a significant site of unrest during the REBELLION OF 1837. The rebellion, which was part of a broader series of armed uprisings in British North America, involved rioting in Montreal itself. An inhabitant of the city at the time wrote about the rebellion, providing a firsthand account of the events. The rebellion in Montreal was marked by civil disorder, with rioting occurring as part of the larger political and armed conflict against the British colonial government. The uprising was driven by grievances over political representation, governance, and ethnic tensions, and Montreal served as a key urban center where these tensions erupted into public violence. The inhabitant's written account likely captured the chaos, fear, and political fervor of the period, offering a personal perspective on the rebellion's impact on the city and its residents. Together, these details highlight Montreal's role as a flashpoint in the 1837 rebellion, where local unrest and rioting were intertwined with the broader struggle for reform and self-determination in Lower Canada.</data>
      <data key="d8">MONTREAL</data>
    </edge>
