    <edge source="LOWER CANADA" target="RUSSELL RESOLUTIONS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Russell Resolutions were a formal set of measures issued by the British government in response to the political and financial grievances raised by the colony of Lower Canada. These resolutions directly addressed the colony's ongoing disputes with the Crown, specifically rejecting key demands made by the reformist majority in the Legislative Assembly. The resolutions were framed as a direct reply to the complaints originating from Lower Canada, which had been escalating over issues of fiscal control, political representation, and colonial governance. By refusing to concede to the principal requestssuch as an elected Legislative Council and full control over the colonial budgetthe Russell Resolutions effectively intensified the constitutional crisis in Lower Canada, contributing to the unrest that preceded the Rebellions of 18371838. In summary, the Russell Resolutions served as the British government's official, and largely negative, response to Lower Canada's demands, reinforcing imperial authority while dismissing the colony's core political and financial reforms.</data>
      <data key="d8">RUSSELL RESOLUTIONS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LOWER CANADA" target="THE RUSSELL RESOLUTIONS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
