    <edge source="PATRIOTE PARTY" target="LOWER CANADA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Patriote Party was a political movement active in Lower Canada during the early 19th century. It operated primarily as a reformist and democratic force, advocating for significant political changes within the colonial framework. The party's core objectives centered on defending French-Canadian rights, promoting democratic governance, and challenging the entrenched British colonial authority. Central to its platform was the support for the Ninety-Two Resolutions, a comprehensive list of grievances and demands presented to the British government, which called for greater local autonomy, responsible government, and the protection of French-Canadian cultural and political interests. Through its activities, the Patriote Party sought to transform Lower Canada's political system, aiming to reduce the power of the British-appointed governor and the oligarchic Chteau Clique, while increasing the influence of the elected assembly. Its efforts ultimately contributed to the broader political tensions that led to the Rebellions of 18371838, marking the party as a pivotal force in the region's struggle for self-determination and constitutional reform.</data>
      <data key="d8">PATRIOTE PARTY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATRIOTE PARTY" target="PAPINEAU" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Papineau was a central figure and leader in the Patriote party.</data>
      <data key="d8">PATRIOTE PARTY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATRIOTE PARTY" target="REBELLION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Patriote Party, led by Louis-Joseph Papineau, was a key political and reformist movement in Lower Canada (present-day Quebec) during the early 19th century. The party was deeply involved in the broader movement toward rebellion, which culminated in the Rebellion of 1837. This uprising was driven by the Patriotes' demands for democratic reforms, greater local autonomy, and resistance to the British colonial government's authoritarian rule. The party's leadership, particularly Papineau, played a central role in organizing and inspiring the rebellion, which ultimately failed but significantly influenced Canadian political development. The Patriote Party's actions and ideology were inseparable from the rebellion, as they sought to address grievances through both political agitation and, ultimately, armed resistance.</data>
      <data key="d8">PATRIOTE PARTY</data>
    </edge>
