    <edge source="ST CHARLES" target="REBELLION OF 1837" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">St. Charles was a significant location during the Rebellion of 1837, a pivotal uprising in Canadian history. The rebellion, which took place in Lower Canada (present-day Quebec), involved a series of armed conflicts between local patriots and British colonial authorities. One of the key engagements of this rebellion occurred at St. Charles, where a battle was fought. This battle was part of the broader insurrection led by Patriote forces seeking political reform and greater self-governance. The conflict at St. Charles was notable not only for its military significance but also for its symbolic impact; the rebellion's outcome at this location encouraged the continuation of the uprising, despite initial setbacks. The events at St. Charles underscored the tensions between the colonial government and the local population, contributing to the larger narrative of the Rebellion of 1837. Ultimately, the rebellion was suppressed, but it played a crucial role in shaping Canadian political evolution, leading to the eventual establishment of responsible government. St. Charles thus stands as a historical marker of resistance and the struggle for democratic rights within the context of the 1837 rebellion.</data>
      <data key="d8">ST CHARLES</data>
    </edge>
