      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Durham, formally known as John George Lambton, 1st Earl of Durham, was a British statesman and colonial administrator who played a pivotal role in the governance of British North America. He was sent to Lower Canada in 1838 to deal with the rebellion that had erupted there, serving as the governor of Lower Canada. In this capacity, he was tasked with investigating the causes of the unrest and restoring order. Durham's time in the colony was marked by his decisive, though controversial, actions, including the exile of rebel leaders and the suspension of the local constitution. His most enduring contribution came from his subsequent report, known as the Durham Report, in which he proposed the legislative union of Upper and Lower Canada. This recommendation was a direct response to the political and ethnic tensions he observed, particularly the conflict between the French-Canadian majority and the British minority. Durham argued that the union would assimilate the French Canadians into British culture and create a more stable, efficient government. His proposals ultimately led to the Act of Union in 1840, which merged the two provinces into the Province of Canada. Durham's legacy is thus deeply tied to both his immediate handling of the Lower Canada rebellion and his long-term vision for Canadian governance, which laid the groundwork for responsible government and eventual confederation.</data>
      <data key="d8">DURHAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DURHAM" target="ENGLAND" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Durham's actions were attacked in England as unconstitutional</data>
      <data key="d8">DURHAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DURHAM" target="CANADA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Lord Durham, also referred to as Durham, served as the Governor of Canada, during which time he issued a formal proclamation in the region. His role as governor placed him in a position of significant authority over Canadian affairs, and his proclamation was an official act issued under that authority. The entity "Canada" refers to the territory under his governance at the time, and "Durham" is the individual who held the gubernatorial office. Together, these descriptions confirm that Durham, holding the title of Lord Durham, acted as the governor of Canada and publicly issued a proclamation while in that capacity. No contradictions exist between the descriptions; they are consistent in identifying the same person, his office, and his official action.</data>
      <data key="d8">DURHAM</data>
    </edge>
