    <edge source="DURHAM" target="THE TIMES" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">John George Lambton, 1st Earl of Durham, commonly referred to as Durham, was the subject of sharp criticism from the British newspaper *The Times*. The newspaper gave Durham the nickname "Lord High Seditioner" in response to a specific proclamation he issued. This epithet was used to characterize Durham's actions or statements as seditious, implying that his proclamation was seen as inciting rebellion or undermining established authority. The nickname was a direct and pointed rebuke from *The Times*, reflecting the newspaper's disapproval of Durham's political stance or conduct at the time. The criticism was notable enough to be recorded in historical accounts, highlighting the tension between Durham and the influential press. The exact content of the proclamation is not detailed, but the nickname suggests it was perceived as radical or inflammatory by the newspaper. This episode underscores the contentious relationship between Durham and the media, and the political climate of the era in which he operated.</data>
      <data key="d8">DURHAM</data>
    </edge>
