    <edge source="ONLINE DISTRIBUTED PROOFREADING TEAM" target="THE INTERNET ARCHIVE/AMERICAN LIBRARIES" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Online Distributed Proofreading Team (DP) is a volunteer-driven initiative that produces high-quality digital texts by coordinating the proofreading of scanned documents. A key source of its source material is The Internet Archive/American Libraries, a vast digital library that provides access to millions of scanned books, manuscripts, and other cultural artifacts. The DP team relies on the image filestypically page scanssupplied by The Internet Archive/American Libraries as the visual basis for its work. These images are then processed through a distributed, multi-step proofreading workflow, where volunteers compare the scanned images against the text that has been automatically generated via optical character recognition (OCR). The teams primary goal is to correct errors in the OCR output, ensuring that the final text is accurate, complete, and faithful to the original printed work. Thus, the relationship between the two entities is direct and functional: The Internet Archive/American Libraries provides the raw image data, and the Online Distributed Proofreading Team uses those images to produce the final, corrected text. This collaboration enables the creation of freely accessible, reliable digital editions of public-domain works, which are then often returned to The Internet Archive for public distribution. In summary, the Online Distributed Proofreading Team depends on The Internet Archive/American Libraries for its source images, and it transforms those images into polished, readable text through a rigorous, community-based proofreading process.</data>
      <data key="d8">THE INTERNET ARCHIVE/AMERICAN LIBRARIES</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ONLINE DISTRIBUTED PROOFREADING TEAM" target="PROJECT GUTENBERG" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Online Distributed Proofreading Team's e-text is hosted by Project Gutenberg.</data>
      <data key="d8">ONLINE DISTRIBUTED PROOFREADING TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ONLINE DISTRIBUTED PROOFREADING TEAM" target="INTERNET ARCHIVE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Online Distributed Proofreading Team (DP) is a volunteer-driven initiative that relies on digital scans of public domain books to produce accurate, freely available e-texts. A key resource in this process is the Internet Archive, a vast digital library that hosts millions of scanned books, documents, and other media. The DP team specifically utilizes page images provided by the Internet Archive as the primary source material for its proofreading workflow. By accessing these high-quality scans, volunteers can compare the original page layout and text against the transcribed digital version, ensuring that errors are caught and corrected with high fidelity. The collaboration between the Online Distributed Proofreading Team and the Internet Archive is essential: the Archive supplies the raw visual data (page images), while the DP team transforms those images into clean, searchable, and reliable electronic texts. This partnership streamlines the digitization process, allowing the DP team to focus on textual accuracy without needing to manage physical book scanning or storage. In essence, the Internet Archive serves as the foundational image repository, and the Online Distributed Proofreading Team acts as the meticulous editorial layer, together producing high-quality e-books for public use.</data>
      <data key="d8">ONLINE DISTRIBUTED PROOFREADING TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ONLINE DISTRIBUTED PROOFREADING TEAM" target="THE INTERNET ARCHIVE" id="0">
