    <edge source="BRACKETT'S BATTALION" target="FIFTH IOWA CAVALRY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">BRACKETT'S BATTALION was a military unit that was formally assigned to the FIFTH IOWA CAVALRY regiment. This assignment indicates that Brackett's Battalion operated as a component or attached formation within the larger Fifth Iowa Cavalry, a regiment of the Union Army during the American Civil War. The battalion, under the command of Colonel A. G. Brackett, served in various campaigns and engagements as part of this cavalry regiment, contributing to the overall strength and operational capacity of the Fifth Iowa Cavalry. The relationship between the two units was one of direct organizational subordination, with Brackett's Battalion functioning under the regimental structure and command of the Fifth Iowa Cavalry. This integration allowed for coordinated tactics, shared logistics, and unified command during military operations. The Fifth Iowa Cavalry, as a whole, was known for its service in the Western Theater, and Brackett's Battalion played a role in that service. The assignment was a permanent or long-term attachment, reflecting the battalion's integral status within the regiment. No contradictory information exists regarding this relationship; both descriptions confirm the same organizational link. Thus, Brackett's Battalion was a distinct but subordinate unit within the Fifth Iowa Cavalry, and its history is intertwined with that of the regiment.</data>
      <data key="d8">BRACKETT'S BATTALION</data>
    </edge>
