    </edge>
    <edge source="MAYAS" target="EGYPT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Mayas and Egyptians are two ancient civilizations that, according to the provided descriptions, shared a remarkable number of identical customs and attainments. These parallels are so extensive that they suggest a direct cultural or historical link between the two groups. Notably, the Egyptians themselves acknowledged this connection, as their priests pointed toward the Westspecifically identifying the region of Mayabas the birthplace of their ancestors. This directional reference implies that the Mayas may have been the progenitors of Egyptian civilization, or at least that the Egyptians believed their roots lay in the Mayan world.

The descriptions emphasize that the Mayas possessed customs and achievements that were virtually indistinguishable from those of the Egyptians. However, a key distinction is noted: the Mayas had certain rites that were entirely unknown to the Egyptians. This suggests that while the two cultures shared a foundational set of practices and knowledge, the Mayas developed or preserved additional unique religious or ceremonial traditions that did not transfer to or survive within Egyptian culture.

The numerous coincidences in attainmentslikely referring to advancements in architecture, astronomy, mathematics, writing, or other intellectual pursuitsand customs are described as linking the two civilizations directly. This direct linkage, combined with the Egyptian priests testimony, reinforces the idea of a shared origin or significant cultural exchange. The consistent theme across all descriptions is the strong, undeniable connection between the Mayas and Egyptians, with the Mayas holding a position of primacy as the ancestral source, while also possessing exclusive rites that set them apart from their Egyptian counterparts.</data>
      <data key="d8">MAYAS</data>
    </edge>
