    <edge source="KHATI" target="EGYPT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Khati are an entity historically associated with Egypt, most notably for their role in obstructing Egyptian military expansion. They are described as having placed significant obstacles in the way of the Egyptians' conquests, suggesting a period of conflict or resistance between the two groups. Additionally, the name "Egypt" is said to have etymological roots in the Maya language, and the Khati are directly linked to this linguistic origin. This connection implies that the Khati may have had cultural or historical ties to the region that influenced the naming of Egypt itself. While the descriptions do not provide further detail on the nature of the Khati's identity or their exact relationship with Egypt, they collectively position the Khati as a people who both resisted Egyptian power and contributed to the foundational naming of the land. The information is sparse but consistent, with no contradictions, and together it paints a picture of the Khati as a historically significant group intertwined with Egypt's early history and nomenclature.</data>
      <data key="d8">KHATI</data>
    </edge>
